window.PYQ_DATA.add({"id":"px-ncert11-bio-cell","kind":"practice","exam":"NEET","year":null,"title":"Cell: The Unit of Life · Practice","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","subject":"Biology","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","marking":{"mcq":[4,-1]},"rules":{},"note":"","questions":[{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-001","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":1,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"New mitochondria in a cell arise by","image":"","options":["de novo assembly from cytoplasmic proteins","fission of pre-existing mitochondria","budding from the Golgi trans face","fusion of lysosomes"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Mitochondria are semi-autonomous organelles with their own DNA and ribosomes.\nThey divide by fission, so new mitochondria always arise from pre-existing ones.\nThey are not assembled de novo, budded from the Golgi or formed by fusion of lysosomes.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-002","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":2,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following statements is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["The ribosomes of a polysome translate the same mRNA into proteins.","A prokaryotic ribosome measures about 15 nm by 20 nm.","Bacterial flagella have the same 9+2 microtubular axoneme as eukaryotic flagella.","Ribosomes in mitochondria and chloroplasts are 70S, like those of prokaryotes."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: the ribosomes of a polysome all translate the same mRNA.\n(2) Correct: NCERT gives the prokaryotic ribosome size as about 15 nm by 20 nm.\n(3) Incorrect: a bacterial flagellum has a filament, hook and basal body. It lacks the 9+2 microtubular axoneme of eukaryotic flagella.\n(4) Correct: mitochondria and chloroplasts have 70S ribosomes, like prokaryotes.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-003","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":3,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"A cell shows the following features under study: no membrane-bound nucleus, 70S ribosomes associated with the plasma membrane, cyanophycean granules lying free in the cytoplasm, gas vacuoles, and pigment-containing membranous extensions called chromatophores. The cell most likely belongs to:","image":"","options":["Mycoplasma","Chlamydomonas","A cyanobacterium (blue-green alga)","Euglena"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"No membrane-bound nucleus and 70S ribosomes on the plasma membrane mean the cell is a prokaryote.\nCyanophycean granules, gas vacuoles and pigment-bearing chromatophores are typical of cyanobacteria (blue-green algae).\nChlamydomonas and Euglena are eukaryotes. They have a nucleus, chloroplasts and 80S cytoplasmic ribosomes.\nMycoplasma is prokaryotic but not photosynthetic, so it lacks chromatophores.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-004","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":4,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about centrioles is incorrect?","image":"","options":["The two centrioles of a centrosome lie perpendicular to each other.","Each centriole has nine evenly spaced peripheral fibrils, each of which is a triplet.","Each centriole has nine peripheral doublets surrounding a pair of central microtubules.","The proteinaceous hub in the proximal region is connected to the peripheral triplets by radial spokes."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: the two centrioles of a centrosome lie perpendicular to each other.\n(2) Correct: each centriole has nine evenly spaced peripheral triplet fibrils in a cartwheel pattern.\n(3) Incorrect: a centriole has no central pair of microtubules. The 9 doublets + 2 central pattern belongs to the axoneme of cilia and flagella.\n(4) Correct: the proteinaceous hub in the proximal region is joined to the peripheral triplets by protein radial spokes.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-005","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":5,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The Na$^+$/K$^+$ pump is an example of active transport across the plasma membrane.\nReason (R): In active transport, ions move along their concentration gradient without the use of ATP.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true: the Na$^+$/K$^+$ pump moves ions using ATP, so it is active transport.\nR is false: active transport moves substances against the concentration gradient (from lower to higher concentration) and uses ATP.\nMovement along the gradient without ATP describes passive transport, not active transport.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-006","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":6,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The Golgi apparatus remains in close association with the endoplasmic reticulum.\nReason (R): Vesicles carrying materials from the ER fuse with the cis face of the Golgi apparatus, and the materials move towards its maturing face.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: NCERT states that the Golgi apparatus remains in close association with the ER.\nR is true: ER-derived vesicles fuse with the cis (forming) face, and their contents move towards the trans (maturing) face.\nR explains A. The Golgi constantly receives material from the ER, which is why the two organelles lie close together.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-007","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":7,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A 70S ribosome is made of a 50S and a 30S subunit, even though 50 + 30 is not equal to 70.\nReason (R): 'S' (Svedberg unit) is a sedimentation coefficient that indirectly reflects density and size, so S values of subunits are not simply additive.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. A prokaryotic 70S ribosome has a 50S large subunit and a 30S small subunit, yet 50 + 30 = 80, not 70.\nR is true. The Svedberg unit (S) is a sedimentation coefficient. It measures how fast a particle settles during centrifugation, which indirectly reflects its size and density, not simply its mass.\nWhen the subunits join, the overall shape and density change, so their S values do not add arithmetically. R therefore explains A correctly.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-008","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":8,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Many bacteria carry small circular DNA molecules in addition to their genomic DNA. Which unique phenotypic character is commonly conferred by this extra DNA?","image":"","options":["Ability to form a nuclear envelope","Resistance to antibiotics","Formation of 80S ribosomes","Ability to take up the Gram stain"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The small circular DNA found outside the bacterial genomic DNA is called a plasmid.\nNCERT notes that plasmids confer certain unique phenotypic characters, such as resistance to antibiotics.\nBacteria have no nuclear envelope and contain 70S ribosomes, not 80S. Gram staining depends on cell wall structure, not on plasmids.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-009","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":9,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Given below are two statements:\nStatement I: Schwann studied animal cells, reported a thin outer layer now known as the plasma membrane, and concluded that a cell wall is a unique character of plant cells.\nStatement II: Rudolf Virchow explained that new cells are formed from pre-existing cells, and this gave the cell theory its final shape.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Schwann studied animal cells and reported a thin outer layer, now called the plasma membrane. From his study of plant tissues, he concluded that a cell wall is unique to plant cells.\nStatement II is correct. Virchow (1855) explained that cells divide and new cells arise from pre-existing cells ('Omnis cellula-e cellula').\nThis idea gave the cell theory its final shape.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-010","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":10,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"A transverse section of a eukaryotic flagellum passes through its axoneme, and another section passes through the basal body from which it emerges. The total number of microtubules seen in the axoneme section and in the basal body section respectively would be","image":"","options":["20 and 27","18 and 27","20 and 29","11 and 9"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Axoneme: it has a 9+2 array, made of nine doublets plus two central microtubules.\nNumber in the axoneme $= 9\\\\times2+2 = 20$.\nBasal body: it is centriole-like, with nine triplets and only a proteinaceous hub at the centre, so it has no central microtubules.\nNumber in the basal body $= 9\\\\times3 = 27$.\nOption (3) wrongly gives the basal body a central pair ($27+2 = 29$).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-011","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":11,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(A) Nucleolus\n(B) Nuclear pore\n(C) Kinetochore\n(D) Satellite\nList-II\n(I) Disc-shaped structure on the sides of the centromere\n(II) Small chromosome fragment set off by a non-staining secondary constriction\n(III) Site of active ribosomal RNA synthesis\n(IV) Passage for two-way movement of RNA and proteins\nChoose the correct answer:","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-I, B-IV, C-III, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A-III: the nucleolus is the site of active rRNA synthesis.\nB-IV: nuclear pores let RNA and proteins move in both directions.\nC-I: kinetochores are disc-shaped structures on the sides of the centromere.\nD-II: a satellite is the small fragment set off by a non-staining secondary constriction.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-012","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":12,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Mitochondria are not easily visible under the light microscope unless specifically stained.\nReason (R): Mitochondria lack their own DNA and ribosomes.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT notes that mitochondria are not easily visible under the microscope unless they are specifically stained.\nR is false. Mitochondria have their own single circular DNA, a few RNA molecules and 70S ribosomes, which makes them semi-autonomous.\nSince R is false, it cannot explain A.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-013","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":13,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(A) Simple diffusion\n(B) Osmosis\n(C) Active transport\n(D) Carrier-assisted passive transport\nList-II\n(i) Movement of water by diffusion\n(ii) Na$^+$/K$^+$ pump using ATP\n(iii) Neutral solutes moving along the concentration gradient\n(iv) Polar molecules crossing with the help of a membrane protein, without energy expenditure\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A-iii, B-i, C-ii, D-iv","A-i, B-iii, C-iv, D-ii","A-iii, B-i, C-iv, D-ii","A-iv, B-i, C-ii, D-iii"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A-iii: neutral solutes cross by simple diffusion, moving along the concentration gradient.\nB-i: osmosis is the diffusion of water across a membrane.\nC-ii: the Na$^+$/K$^+$ pump uses ATP to move ions against their gradient, which is active transport.\nD-iv: polar molecules cross passively with the help of a carrier protein, without using energy.\nThe combination A-iii, B-i, C-ii, D-iv is option (1).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-014","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":14,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"In a prokaryotic cell, the ribosomes are associated with the:","image":"","options":["Rough endoplasmic reticulum","Nuclear envelope","Plasma membrane","Golgi apparatus"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT fact: prokaryotes have no membrane-bound organelles such as the ER, nuclear envelope or Golgi apparatus.\nTheir ribosomes are therefore associated with the plasma membrane.\nRough ER is the tempting wrong option. Ribosomes attached to the ER, and to the nuclear envelope, are features of eukaryotic cells only.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-015","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":15,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"A chromosome whose centromere is located at its very end is called:","image":"","options":["Acrocentric","Sub-metacentric","Metacentric","Telocentric"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Chromosomes are classified by where the centromere lies.\nA telocentric chromosome has its centromere at the very end (terminal position).\nThe tempting wrong option is acrocentric: there the centromere is close to the end but not at it, giving one extremely short arm and one very long arm.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-016","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":16,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\n(A) Amyloplast\n(B) Aleuroplast\n(C) Chromoplast\n(D) Chloroplast\nList-II\n(I) Stores proteins\n(II) Contains chlorophyll and carotenoids that trap light energy\n(III) Stores starch, e.g., in potato\n(IV) Contains fat-soluble carotene and xanthophylls giving yellow, orange or red colour\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-I, B-III, C-IV, D-II","A-III, B-I, C-II, D-IV","A-IV, B-I, C-III, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A to III: amyloplasts store starch, as in potato ('amylo' means starch).\nB to I: aleuroplasts store proteins.\nC to IV: chromoplasts contain fat-soluble carotene and xanthophylls, giving yellow, orange or red colour.\nD to II: chloroplasts contain chlorophyll and carotenoids that trap light energy.\nThe correct match is A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-017","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":17,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (bacterial structure) with List-II (feature/function):\nList-I\nA. Glycocalyx\nB. Cell wall\nC. Mesosome\nD. Chromatophore\nList-II\n(i) Determines cell shape and prevents the bacterium from bursting or collapsing\n(ii) Pigment-containing membranous extension seen in cyanobacteria\n(iii) Outermost layer of the cell envelope; may be a slime layer or a capsule\n(iv) Infolding of plasma membrane that increases its surface area and enzymatic content\nChoose the correct option:","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(ii)","A-(i), B-(iii), C-(iv), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(i), C-(ii), D-(iv)","A-(iv), B-(i), C-(iii), D-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A. Glycocalyx is the outermost layer of the cell envelope, either a loose slime layer or a tough capsule → (iii).\nB. The cell wall determines cell shape and gives strong support that prevents bursting or collapsing → (i).\nC. The mesosome is an infolding of the plasma membrane that increases surface area and enzymatic content → (iv).\nD. The chromatophore is a pigment-containing membranous extension in cyanobacteria → (ii).\nSo the correct match is A-(iii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(ii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-018","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":18,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Mitochondria and chloroplasts are not considered part of the endomembrane system.\nReason (R): Mitochondria and chloroplasts are not bound by membranes.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. The endomembrane system includes the ER, Golgi complex, lysosomes and vacuoles.\nMitochondria and chloroplasts are excluded because their functions are not coordinated with these organelles.\nR is false. Mitochondria and chloroplasts are both double membrane-bound organelles.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-019","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":19,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I (Scientist)\nA. Matthias Schleiden\nB. Theodore Schwann\nC. Rudolf Virchow\nD. Robert Brown\nList-II (Contribution)\nI. Discovered the nucleus\nII. New cells are formed from pre-existing cells\nIII. All plants are composed of different kinds of cells that form tissues\nIV. Concluded that the presence of a cell wall is a unique character of plant cells\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A. Schleiden (botanist) → III: plants are made of different kinds of cells that form tissues.\nB. Schwann → IV: he concluded that a cell wall is a unique character of plant cells.\nC. Virchow → II: new cells arise from pre-existing cells (Omnis cellula-e cellula).\nD. Robert Brown → I: discovered the nucleus.\nSo the correct match is A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-020","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":20,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about lysosomes are correct?\n(i) They are formed by the process of packaging in the Golgi apparatus.\n(ii) They are rich in lipases, proteases and carbohydrases.\n(iii) Their enzymes work best at alkaline pH.\n(iv) They are membrane-bound vesicular structures.\n(v) Their enzymes can digest nucleic acids.","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: lysosomes form by packaging in the Golgi apparatus.\n(ii) Correct: they are rich in hydrolases such as lipases, proteases and carbohydrases.\n(iii) Incorrect: these enzymes work best at acidic pH, not alkaline pH.\n(iv) Correct: lysosomes are membrane-bound vesicular structures.\n(v) Correct: lysosomal enzymes can digest nucleic acids, as well as proteins, lipids and carbohydrates.\nFour statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-021","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":21,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"A prokaryote is found to lack the middle layer of the typical three-layered bacterial cell envelope. Which of the following is most consistent with NCERT?","image":"","options":["It must be a cyanobacterium, since chromatophores replace the cell wall","It is likely a mycoplasma, and it lacks the structure that determines cell shape and protects against bursting","It must lack a plasma membrane as well, since the envelope layers act as a single unit","It will necessarily be Gram positive because the stain binds directly to the membrane"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Key fact: The bacterial envelope has three layers: the outer glycocalyx, the middle cell wall and the inner plasma membrane.\nMissing middle layer: A prokaryote without a cell wall is most likely a mycoplasma. It therefore lacks the structure that gives shape and prevents bursting.\nOption (1) is wrong because chromatophores are internal membranes, not a replacement for the wall. Option (3) is wrong because the plasma membrane is still present. Option (4) is wrong because Gram staining depends on the cell wall.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-022","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":22,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Given below are two statements.\nStatement I: Based on his studies of plant tissues, Schwann concluded that the presence of a cell wall is a unique character of plant cells.\nStatement II: Rudolf Virchow explained that new cells are formed from pre-existing cells (Omnis cellula-e cellula).\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Schwann studied animal cells, but NCERT states that, based on his studies of plant tissues, he concluded the cell wall is a unique character of plant cells.\nStatement II is correct. Rudolf Virchow (1855) explained that new cells form from pre-existing cells (Omnis cellula-e cellula). This modified the hypothesis of Schleiden and Schwann and gave the cell theory its final shape.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-023","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":23,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following locations contain 70S ribosomes?\n(a) Mitochondrial matrix\n(b) Chloroplast stroma\n(c) Rough ER of an animal cell\n(d) Cytoplasm of a bacterial cell\n(e) Cytoplasm of a yeast cell","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(a) Mitochondrial matrix: 70S. ✓\n(b) Chloroplast stroma: 70S. ✓\n(c) Rough ER of an animal cell: 80S, because these are eukaryotic cytoplasmic ribosomes. ✗\n(d) Bacterial cytoplasm: 70S, since bacteria are prokaryotes. ✓\n(e) Yeast cytoplasm: 80S, because yeast is a eukaryotic fungus. ✗\nThree locations contain 70S ribosomes.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-024","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":24,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The primary wall of a young plant cell is capable of growth.\nReason (R): The secondary wall is laid down on the outer side of the primary wall as the cell matures.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. The primary wall of a young cell can grow, and this ability gradually decreases as the cell matures.\nR is false. The secondary wall is laid down on the inner side of the primary wall, towards the plasma membrane, not on the outer side.\nSo A is true and R is false.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-025","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":25,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"A human red blood cell is about 7.0 $\\mu m$ in diameter, and a Mycoplasma cell is about 0.3 $\\mu m$ long. Roughly how many Mycoplasma cells, placed end to end, would span the diameter of one RBC?","image":"","options":["About 2","About 23","About 230","About 2300"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: RBC diameter = 7.0 $\\mu m$; Mycoplasma length = 0.3 $\\mu m$.\nFormula: number of cells = RBC diameter ÷ length of one Mycoplasma.\nSubstituting: $\\frac{7.0\\ \\mu m}{0.3\\ \\mu m} \\approx 23.3$. Both values are already in $\\mu m$, so no unit conversion is needed.\nSo about 23 Mycoplasma cells would span one RBC. Options of 230 or 2300 come from mixing up units.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-026","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":26,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following statements comparing mitochondria and chloroplasts is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Both are double membrane-bound and possess circular DNA and 70S ribosomes","The number of chloroplasts ranges from one per cell in Chlamydomonas to 20-40 per mesophyll cell","In mitochondria, the inner compartment enclosed by the inner membrane is filled with the matrix, while the outer compartment lies between the two membranes","The inner membrane of the chloroplast folds into cristae that bear chlorophyll pigments"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: both organelles have two membranes, circular DNA and 70S ribosomes.\n(2) Correct: there is one chloroplast per cell in Chlamydomonas and 20–40 per mesophyll cell.\n(3) Correct: this matches the NCERT description of mitochondrial compartments.\n(4) Incorrect: cristae are infoldings of the inner mitochondrial membrane. In chloroplasts, chlorophyll is located on the thylakoids.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-027","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":27,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Different cells have different sizes. Arrange the following cells in an ascending order of their size. Choose the correct option among the followings\ni. Mycoplasma\nii. Ostrich eggs\niii. Human RBC\niv. Bacteria","image":"","options":["i, iv, iii & ii","i, iii, iv & ii","ii, i, iii & iv","iii, ii, i & iv"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given approximate sizes:\nMycoplasma ≈ $0.3\\ \\mu m$ (smallest cell)\nTypical bacteria ≈ $3$–$5\\ \\mu m$\nHuman RBC ≈ $7\\ \\mu m$\nOstrich egg: the largest single cell\nAscending order: i < iv < iii < ii.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 8 (MCQ Q5)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-028","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":28,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"The Na$^+$/K$^+$ pump in the plasma membrane is an example of:","image":"","options":["Simple diffusion","Osmosis","Passive transport through a carrier protein","Active transport using ATP"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Key fact: the Na$^+$/K$^+$ pump moves Na$^+$ out of the cell and K$^+$ into it.\nBoth ions are moved against their concentration gradients.\nMoving a substance against its gradient needs energy, which is supplied by ATP. This is active transport.\nOption (3) is tempting because the pump is a carrier protein. However, passive carrier transport works only down a gradient, so it cannot explain the pump.\nSimple diffusion and osmosis are passive and need no carrier, so they fail too.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-029","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":29,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Some bacteria attach to rocks in streams and to host tissues using small bristle-like fibres that sprout out of the cell. These fibres are:","image":"","options":["Flagella","Mesosomes","Fimbriae","Chromatophores"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT fact: fimbriae are small bristle-like fibres sprouting out of the cell.\nIn some bacteria they help attachment to rocks in streams and to host tissues, which matches the question.\nFlagella are the tempting wrong option, but they are long structures used for motility, not attachment.\nMesosomes and chromatophores are internal membrane extensions, not surface fibres.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-030","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":30,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In plant cells, the concentration of many ions is significantly higher in the vacuole than in the surrounding cytoplasm.\nStatement II: Ions enter the plant vacuole by simple diffusion across the tonoplast, without any expenditure of energy.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. NCERT notes that ion concentration in the vacuole is significantly higher than in the cytoplasm.\nStatement II is incorrect. To build up this higher concentration, the tonoplast moves ions against their concentration gradient.\nMovement against a gradient is active transport and needs energy (ATP). Simple diffusion only moves substances down a gradient.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-031","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":31,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(A) Amyloplasts\n(B) Elaioplasts\n(C) Aleuroplasts\n(D) Chromoplasts\nList-II\n(I) Store proteins\n(II) Contain fat-soluble carotenoid pigments\n(III) Store starch\n(IV) Store oils and fats\nChoose the correct answer.","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-I, B-IV, C-III, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A, amyloplasts: store starch (e.g., potato), so A-III.\nB, elaioplasts: store oils and fats, so B-IV.\nC, aleuroplasts: store proteins, so C-I.\nD, chromoplasts: contain fat-soluble carotenoid pigments such as carotene and xanthophylls, so D-II.\nThe correct match is A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-032","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":32,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"The phrase 'Omnis cellula-e cellula', meaning that new cells arise only from pre-existing cells, was given by:","image":"","options":["Matthias Schleiden","Theodore Schwann","Rudolf Virchow","Robert Brown"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Key fact: in 1855 Rudolf Virchow explained that cells divide, and that new cells form only from pre-existing cells. He summed this up as 'Omnis cellula-e cellula'.\nSchleiden and Schwann proposed the original cell theory, but they did not explain how new cells arise. This is why their names are the tempting wrong choices.\nRobert Brown is known for discovering the nucleus.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-033","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":33,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the following statements about prokaryotic cells:\n(a) Mycoplasma is the prokaryote that lacks a cell wall.\n(b) Inclusion bodies such as glycogen granules are enclosed by a single membrane.\n(c) In cyanobacteria, chromatophores are pigment-containing membranous extensions into the cytoplasm.\n(d) The bacterial flagellum has a filament, a hook and a basal body, and the filament is its longest part.\n(e) Plasmid DNA is separated from the cytoplasm by a nuclear membrane.\nChoose the option with only the correct statements.","image":"","options":["(a), (b) and (c) only","(c), (d) and (e) only","(a), (c) and (d) only","(a) and (d) only"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(a) Correct: mycoplasma is the only prokaryote without a cell wall.\n(b) Incorrect: inclusion bodies lie free in the cytoplasm and are not bound by any membrane.\n(c) Correct: chromatophores are pigment-containing membranous extensions of the plasma membrane in cyanobacteria.\n(d) Correct: the bacterial flagellum has a filament, a hook and a basal body, and the filament is the longest part.\n(e) Incorrect: prokaryotes have no nuclear membrane at all.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-034","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":34,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"In a squash of a dividing cell, a student observes a chromosome with the following features:\n- its primary constriction lies slightly away from the middle, giving one shorter and one longer arm;\n- near the tip of one arm, a non-staining constriction at a constant location makes a small terminal fragment appear separate.\nWhich interpretation is correct?","image":"","options":["Sub-metacentric chromosome; the small fragment is a satellite formed due to a secondary constriction","Acrocentric chromosome; the small fragment is the kinetochore","Metacentric chromosome; the small fragment is a satellite formed due to the primary constriction","Sub-metacentric chromosome; the small fragment is a nucleolus attached to the arm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A centromere (primary constriction) slightly away from the middle gives one shorter and one longer arm, so the chromosome is sub-metacentric. An acrocentric chromosome has the centromere very close to one end.\nA non-staining secondary constriction at a constant location cuts off a small terminal fragment called a satellite.\nThe kinetochore is a disc at the sides of the centromere, not a terminal piece. The primary constriction does not form satellites.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-035","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":35,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Vesicles carrying materials from the ER first fuse with which face of the Golgi apparatus, and what is its shape?","image":"","options":["Trans face, which is concave","Trans face, which is convex","Cis face, which is concave","Cis face, which is convex"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Key fact: the Golgi stack has two distinct faces.\nThe cis face, or forming face, is convex. The trans face, or maturing face, is concave.\nVesicles from the ER fuse first with the cis (forming) face.\nProcessed materials then leave from the trans (maturing) face.\nSo the correct choice is the cis face, which is convex. Options naming the trans face, or a concave cis face, mix up the two faces.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-036","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":36,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Besides polysaccharides, the cell walls of some algae contain which mineral?","image":"","options":["Calcium pectate","Sodium chloride","Potassium nitrate","Calcium carbonate"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"NCERT fact: the algal cell wall is made of cellulose, galactans, mannans and minerals such as calcium carbonate.\nSo the mineral asked for is calcium carbonate.\nCalcium pectate is the tempting option, but it is the main component of the middle lamella. It is not a mineral deposit of the algal wall.\nSodium chloride and potassium nitrate are not wall components.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-037","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":37,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Mitochondria are called the 'power houses' of the cell.\nReason (R): Mitochondria are the sites of aerobic respiration and produce cellular energy in the form of ATP.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: mitochondria are called the 'power houses' of the cell.\nR is true: aerobic respiration takes place in mitochondria and releases energy as ATP.\nR explains A. Mitochondria earn the name 'power houses' because they make ATP through aerobic respiration.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-038","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":38,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\n(A) Contractile vacuole\n(B) Food vacuole\n(C) Lysosome\n(D) Golgi apparatus\nList-II\n(I) Hydrolases optimally active at acidic pH\n(II) Formation of glycoproteins and glycolipids\n(III) Osmoregulation and excretion in Amoeba\n(IV) Formed by engulfing food particles in protists","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-I, B-IV, C-III, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A–III: the contractile vacuole carries out osmoregulation and excretion in Amoeba.\nB–IV: a food vacuole forms when a protist engulfs food particles.\nC–I: lysosomes contain hydrolases that work best at acidic pH.\nD–II: the Golgi apparatus is the important site for forming glycoproteins and glycolipids.\nThe matching is A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-039","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":39,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (cell) with List-II (size feature) and choose the correct option.\nList-I:\nA. Mycoplasma\nB. Bacterium\nC. Human red blood cell\nD. Nerve cell\nList-II:\nI. About 7.0 $\\mu m$ in diameter\nII. About 0.3 $\\mu m$ in length\nIII. 3 to 5 $\\mu m$\nIV. Among the longest cells","image":"","options":["A-II, B-III, C-I, D-IV","A-III, B-II, C-I, D-IV","A-II, B-I, C-III, D-IV","A-I, B-III, C-II, D-IV"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A. Mycoplasma → II: the smallest cells, only about 0.3 $\\mu m$ long.\nB. Bacterium → III: typically 3 to 5 $\\mu m$.\nC. Human RBC → I: about 7.0 $\\mu m$ in diameter.\nD. Nerve cell → IV: among the longest cells.\nThis gives A-II, B-III, C-I, D-IV. Option (2) wrongly swaps the Mycoplasma and bacterium sizes.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-040","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":40,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Polar molecules require a carrier protein of the membrane to be transported across the plasma membrane.\nReason (R): Polar molecules cannot pass through the nonpolar lipid bilayer.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"R is true: the interior of the bilayer is nonpolar (hydrophobic), so polar molecules cannot dissolve in it and pass through.\nA is true: polar molecules therefore need a carrier protein to be transported across the membrane.\nR is the correct explanation of A, because the hydrophobic barrier is exactly why a carrier is required.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-041","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":41,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Cilia work like oars and can move either the cell or the fluid surrounding it.\nStatement II: Flagella of prokaryotic bacteria are structurally identical to eukaryotic flagella.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. NCERT describes cilia as small structures that work like oars, moving either the cell or the fluid around it.\nStatement II is incorrect. Bacteria do have flagella, but these are structurally different from eukaryotic flagella. Eukaryotic flagella have a 9+2 microtubular axoneme covered by the plasma membrane, which bacterial flagella lack.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-042","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":42,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"The movement of water across the plasma membrane by diffusion, from a region of its higher concentration to a region of its lower concentration, is called:","image":"","options":["Active transport","Osmosis","Endocytosis","Facilitated transport"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Key fact: osmosis is the diffusion of water across a membrane, from higher to lower water concentration.\nIt is passive and needs no energy, which matches the description exactly.\nActive transport and endocytosis both need ATP, so they do not fit.\nFacilitated transport is the tempting option, but it refers to solutes moving down their gradient with the help of carrier or channel proteins. It is not the specific term for water diffusion.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-043","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":43,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about prokaryotic cells is incorrect?","image":"","options":["Mesosomes help in cell wall formation, DNA replication and distribution to daughter cells","Prokaryotic ribosomes are associated with the plasma membrane and are made of 50S and 30S subunits","Inclusion bodies such as glycogen and phosphate granules are enclosed by a single membrane","In cyanobacteria, chromatophores are membranous extensions into the cytoplasm that contain pigments"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: mesosomes help in cell wall formation, DNA replication and distribution to daughter cells.\n(2) Correct: prokaryotic ribosomes are associated with the plasma membrane and are made of 50S + 30S subunits (70S).\n(3) Incorrect: inclusion bodies such as phosphate granules and glycogen granules store reserve material. They lie free in the cytoplasm and are not bound by any membrane.\n(4) Correct: chromatophores in cyanobacteria are pigment-containing membranous extensions into the cytoplasm.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-044","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":44,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) Chromatin contains DNA, histones, some non-histone proteins and RNA.\n(ii) A single human cell has roughly two metres of DNA distributed among its 46 chromosomes.\n(iii) Kinetochores can be clearly seen in the chromatin of an interphase nucleus.\n(iv) Secondary constrictions are non-staining and occur at a constant location.\n(v) Microbodies are non-membrane bound granules.\n(vi) Microbodies occur only in plant cells.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: chromatin contains DNA, histones, some non-histone proteins and RNA.\n(ii) Correct: a human cell has about 2 m of DNA spread over 46 chromosomes.\n(iii) Incorrect: in interphase, chromatin is a loose network. Chromosomes and their kinetochores are distinct only in dividing cells.\n(iv) Correct: secondary constrictions are non-staining and occur at a constant location.\n(v) Incorrect: microbodies are membrane-bound vesicles.\n(vi) Incorrect: microbodies occur in both plant and animal cells.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-045","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":45,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following membrane-bound organelles is NOT considered part of the endomembrane system?","image":"","options":["Lysosome","Golgi complex","Peroxisome","Vacuole"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT places the ER, Golgi complex, lysosomes and vacuoles in the endomembrane system because their functions are coordinated.\nMitochondria, chloroplasts and peroxisomes are also membrane-bound. However, their functions are not coordinated with these organelles, so they are excluded.\nLysosomes and vacuoles are tempting choices because they are small, but both belong to the system.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-046","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":46,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"A sectional diagram of a chloroplast shows three labelled regions:\nX – the space bounded by the inner membrane\nY – flattened membranous sacs piled like stacks of coins\nZ – flat membranous tubules joining the stacks Y to one another\nWhich of the following statements is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["X contains enzymes needed for synthesis of carbohydrates and proteins","X contains small double-stranded circular DNA molecules","The ribosomes present in X are of the 80S type, like cytoplasmic ribosomes","Y are the grana and Z are the stroma lamellae"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"X, the space bounded by the inner membrane, is the stroma. It contains enzymes for carbohydrate and protein synthesis and small double-stranded circular DNA, so (1) and (2) are correct.\nThe stroma's ribosomes are 70S, which are smaller than the 80S cytoplasmic ribosomes. Statement (3) is therefore incorrect.\nY, the stacks of thylakoids, are grana. Z, the tubules joining them, are stroma lamellae. So (4) is correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-047","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":47,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Membrane proteins are classified as integral or peripheral on the basis of how easily they can be extracted from the membrane.\nStatement II: Peripheral proteins are totally buried within the lipid bilayer, whereas integral proteins lie only on its surface.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. NCERT classifies membrane proteins as integral or peripheral on the basis of how easily they can be extracted from the membrane.\nStatement II is incorrect because it reverses the definitions.\nPeripheral proteins actually lie on the surface of the membrane.\nIntegral proteins are partially or totally buried in the lipid bilayer.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-048","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":48,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about the centrosome and centrioles are correct?\n(i) The two centrioles of a centrosome lie parallel to each other.\n(ii) Each centriole has nine evenly spaced peripheral fibrils of tubulin, each fibril being a triplet.\n(iii) Centrioles are surrounded by amorphous pericentriolar material.\n(iv) In animal cells, centrioles give rise to the spindle fibres during cell division.\n(v) Adjacent peripheral triplets of a centriole are not linked to each other.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Incorrect: the two centrioles of a centrosome lie perpendicular to each other, not parallel.\n(ii) Correct: each centriole has nine evenly spaced peripheral fibrils of tubulin, and each fibril is a triplet.\n(iii) Correct: the centrioles are surrounded by amorphous pericentriolar material.\n(iv) Correct: in animal cells, centrioles form the basal body of cilia and flagella and give rise to spindle fibres.\n(v) Incorrect: adjacent peripheral triplets are linked to each other.\nSo 3 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-049","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":49,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Rudolf Virchow modified the hypothesis of Schleiden and Schwann and gave the cell theory its final shape.\nReason (R): The cell theory formulated by Schleiden and Schwann did not explain how new cells are formed.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: Virchow (1855) modified the hypothesis of Schleiden and Schwann and gave the cell theory its final shape.\nR is true: the original theory did not explain how new cells are formed.\nR explains A: Virchow filled exactly this gap by showing that cells divide and new cells come from pre-existing cells.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-050","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":50,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(a) Anything less than a complete structure of a cell does not ensure independent living.\n(b) As originally formulated by Schleiden and Schwann, the cell theory explained how new cells are formed.\n(c) According to the cell theory as understood today, living organisms are composed of cells and products of cells.\n(d) The cell is the structural, but not the functional, unit of living organisms.\n(e) The invention of the microscope and its improvement leading to the electron microscope revealed the structural details of the cell.","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(a) Correct: only a complete cell ensures independent living.\n(b) Incorrect: the original theory did not explain the formation of new cells. Virchow explained it later.\n(c) Correct: this is part of the modern cell theory.\n(d) Incorrect: the cell is both the structural and the functional unit of life.\n(e) Correct: microscopes, and later the electron microscope, revealed the structural details of the cell.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-051","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":51,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following is the longest portion of a bacterial flagellum, extending from the cell surface to the outside?","image":"","options":["Basal body","Hook","Filament","Pilus"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A bacterial flagellum has three parts: filament, hook and basal body.\nThe filament is the longest part and extends from the cell surface to the outside.\nThe basal body anchors the flagellum in the cell envelope, and the hook joins the filament to the basal body.\nA pilus is a separate surface structure, not part of the flagellum.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-052","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":52,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"In a mesophyll cell of a green leaf, identify the ribosome type found at each location in the order: free in cytoplasm, mitochondrial matrix, chloroplast stroma, attached to rough ER.","image":"","options":["80S, 80S, 70S, 80S","80S, 70S, 70S, 80S","70S, 70S, 70S, 80S","80S, 70S, 80S, 70S"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Eukaryotic ribosomes in the cytoplasm are 80S, whether they float free or sit on the rough ER.\nMitochondria and chloroplasts are semi-autonomous organelles with their own prokaryote-like 70S ribosomes, found in the matrix and the stroma respectively.\nSo the order free cytoplasm, mitochondrial matrix, chloroplast stroma, RER is 80S, 70S, 70S, 80S.\nTrap: assuming every ribosome in a eukaryotic cell is 80S, as in option (1).\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-053","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":53,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\n(A) Mesosome\n(B) Tonoplast\n(C) Kinetochore\n(D) Hub\nList-II\n(i) Disc-shaped structure on the sides of the centromere\n(ii) Proteinaceous central part of the proximal region of a centriole\n(iii) Infolding of plasma membrane in prokaryotes\n(iv) Single membrane bounding the vacuole\nChoose the correct answer:","image":"","options":["A-iii, B-iv, C-i, D-ii","A-iii, B-i, C-iv, D-ii","A-iv, B-iii, C-ii, D-i","A-ii, B-iv, C-i, D-iii"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A–iii: The mesosome is an infolding of the plasma membrane in prokaryotes.\nB–iv: The tonoplast is the single membrane that bounds the vacuole.\nC–i: Kinetochores are disc-shaped structures on the sides of the centromere.\nD–ii: The hub is the proteinaceous central part of the proximal region of a centriole, linked to the peripheral triplets by radial spokes.\nThis gives the sequence A-iii, B-iv, C-i, D-ii.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-054","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":54,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Membranous extensions into the cytoplasm that contain pigments are found in prokaryotes such as cyanobacteria. What are these structures called?","image":"","options":["Mesosomes","Inclusion bodies","Chromatophores","Fimbriae"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Key fact: In some prokaryotes, such as cyanobacteria, the plasma membrane extends into the cytoplasm as membranous structures that contain pigments. These are called chromatophores.\nWhy not mesosomes: Mesosomes are also membrane infoldings, but they help in respiration, secretion and DNA replication. They do not carry pigments.\nInclusion bodies store reserve material and are not bound by a membrane system. Fimbriae are surface bristles used for attachment.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-055","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":55,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are listed by NCERT as constituents of chromatin?\nDNA, histones, non-histone proteins, RNA, cellulose, calcium pectate","image":"","options":["3","5","2","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"NCERT lists chromatin as DNA plus basic proteins called histones, some non-histone proteins and also RNA.\nCount the listed items: DNA, histones, non-histone proteins and RNA make 4.\nCellulose is a cell wall component, and calcium pectate forms the middle lamella. Neither is part of chromatin.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-056","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":56,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"The two subunits of a eukaryotic 80S ribosome are:","image":"","options":["50S and 30S","60S and 20S","60S and 40S","40S and 30S"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The eukaryotic 80S ribosome has a larger 60S subunit and a smaller 40S subunit.\nS (Svedberg) values do not simply add up, which is why $60S + 40S$ gives 80S.\n50S and 30S are the subunits of the prokaryotic 70S ribosome, the tempting wrong option.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-057","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":57,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following mature cells lacks a nucleus?","image":"","options":["Mesophyll cell of a leaf","Sieve tube cell of a vascular plant","Columnar epithelial cell","White blood cell"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT gives two examples of mature cells without a nucleus: the erythrocytes of many mammals and the sieve tube cells of vascular plants.\nMesophyll cells, columnar epithelial cells and white blood cells all keep their nuclei. A WBC may look tempting because RBCs lack a nucleus, but WBCs are nucleated.\nSo the sieve tube cell is the mature cell without a nucleus.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-058","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":58,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Of the two membranes of the chloroplast, the inner membrane is relatively less permeable.\nStatement II: Stroma lamellae are flat membranous tubules that connect thylakoids of different grana.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. NCERT states that the inner membrane of the chloroplast is relatively less permeable than the outer one.\nStatement II is correct. Stroma lamellae are flat membranous tubules that connect the thylakoids of different grana.\nBoth statements are therefore correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-059","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":59,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the following statements about prokaryotic cells:\nStatement I: Pili and fimbriae are surface structures of bacteria that help in motility, and fimbriae may help bacteria attach to rocks in streams.\nStatement II: Inclusion bodies such as phosphate granules, cyanophycean granules and glycogen granules lie free in the cytoplasm and are not bound by any membrane system.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is incorrect. Pili and fimbriae do not help in motility; only flagella move the cell. The point that fimbriae help bacteria attach to rocks in streams and to host tissues is true, but the motility claim makes the statement wrong.\nStatement II is correct. Phosphate, cyanophycean and glycogen granules are inclusion bodies. They lie free in the cytoplasm and are not bound by any membrane.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-060","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":60,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Ribosomes are non-membrane bound organelles found in both prokaryotic and eukaryotic cells.\nStatement II: The centrosome of animal cells is a membrane bound organelle that helps in cell division.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Ribosomes have no membrane around them. They are found in all cells: 70S in prokaryotes and 80S in the cytoplasm of eukaryotes.\nStatement II is incorrect. The centrosome does help in cell division, because its centrioles form the basal bodies and spindle apparatus. However, it is a non-membrane bound organelle made of two cylindrical centrioles.\nSo Statement I is correct and Statement II is incorrect.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-061","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":61,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"A section passes through the common boundary of two adjacent mature plant cells, P and Q, at a point where there are no plasmodesmata. Which sequence correctly gives the layers met while moving from the cytoplasm of cell P to the cytoplasm of cell Q?","image":"","options":["Plasma membrane (P) → primary wall (P) → secondary wall (P) → middle lamella → secondary wall (Q) → primary wall (Q) → plasma membrane (Q)","Primary wall (P) → plasma membrane (P) → middle lamella → plasma membrane (Q) → primary wall (Q)","Plasma membrane (P) → middle lamella → secondary wall (P) → primary wall (Q) → plasma membrane (Q)","Plasma membrane (P) → secondary wall (P) → primary wall (P) → middle lamella → primary wall (Q) → secondary wall (Q) → plasma membrane (Q)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"The secondary wall is deposited inside the primary wall, so it lies next to the plasma membrane.\nThe middle lamella cements the primary walls of the two cells together.\nGoing from P to Q, the order is: membrane (P) → secondary wall (P) → primary wall (P) → middle lamella → primary wall (Q) → secondary wall (Q) → membrane (Q).\nOption (1) is the trap: it wrongly puts the primary wall next to the membrane.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-062","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":62,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\nA. Capsule\nB. Cell wall\nC. Plasma membrane\nD. Mesosome\nList-II\ni. Selectively permeable layer that interacts with the outside world\nii. Infolding of membrane that helps in respiration and DNA replication\niii. Determines cell shape and prevents the bacterium from bursting or collapsing\niv. Thick and tough form of glycocalyx\nChoose the correct answer:","image":"","options":["A-iv, B-iii, C-i, D-ii","A-iii, B-iv, C-ii, D-i","A-iv, B-i, C-iii, D-ii","A-ii, B-iii, C-i, D-iv"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A–iv: The capsule is the thick, tough form of glycocalyx.\nB–iii: The cell wall determines cell shape and gives structural support, preventing the bacterium from bursting or collapsing.\nC–i: The plasma membrane is selectively permeable and interacts with the outside world.\nD–ii: The mesosome is an infolding of the plasma membrane that helps in respiration and DNA replication.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-063","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":63,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are present in the mitochondrial matrix?\n(i) Single circular DNA molecule\n(ii) A few RNA molecules\n(iii) 70S ribosomes\n(iv) Components required for protein synthesis\n(v) 80S ribosomes\n(vi) Chlorophyll","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Five","Four"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(i) Present: the matrix has a single circular DNA molecule.\n(ii) Present: it also has a few RNA molecules.\n(iii) Present: its ribosomes are 70S.\n(iv) Present: it holds the components needed for protein synthesis.\n(v) Absent: 80S ribosomes are found in the eukaryotic cytoplasm, not in mitochondria.\n(vi) Absent: chlorophyll occurs only in chloroplasts.\nSo four items are present.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-064","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":64,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about prokaryotic cells are correct?\n(a) Mesosomes help in respiration and secretion processes.\n(b) The genetic material of prokaryotes is enclosed by a nuclear membrane.\n(c) Plasmid DNA is used to monitor bacterial transformation with foreign DNA.\n(d) Mycoplasma lacks a cell wall around its cell membrane.\n(e) The three layers of the bacterial cell envelope function independently and do not act as a single protective unit.\n(f) Bacteria that take up the Gram stain are called Gram positive.","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(a) Correct: mesosomes help in respiration, secretion, cell wall formation and DNA replication.\n(b) Incorrect: prokaryotic DNA is naked, with no nuclear membrane.\n(c) Correct: plasmid DNA is used to monitor bacterial transformation with foreign DNA.\n(d) Correct: Mycoplasma has no cell wall.\n(e) Incorrect: the glycocalyx, cell wall and plasma membrane do different jobs but act together as a single protective unit.\n(f) Correct: bacteria that take up the Gram stain are Gram positive.\nFour statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-065","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":65,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"In a typical bacterial cell envelope, which sequence correctly lists the three layers from the outside inward?","image":"","options":["Cell wall → glycocalyx → plasma membrane","Glycocalyx → cell wall → plasma membrane","Plasma membrane → cell wall → glycocalyx","Glycocalyx → plasma membrane → cell wall"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The bacterial cell envelope is a tightly bound three-layered structure.\nThe outermost layer is the glycocalyx, either a slime layer or a capsule.\nThe middle layer is the cell wall.\nThe innermost layer is the plasma membrane, which encloses the cytoplasm.\nSo the order from outside inward is glycocalyx → cell wall → plasma membrane. Putting the cell wall outside the glycocalyx, as in option (1), is the common error.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-066","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":66,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the nucleus is correct?","image":"","options":["The nucleolus is a membrane-bound structure and is the site of active ribosomal RNA synthesis","Nuclear pores are formed by fusion of the two nuclear membranes and allow movement of RNA and proteins in both directions","Mature sieve tube cells and erythrocytes of most mammals possess a single prominent nucleus","The outer nuclear membrane is usually continuous with the Golgi apparatus and lacks ribosomes"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Wrong: the nucleolus is not membrane-bound, although it is the site of active rRNA synthesis.\n(2) Correct: nuclear pores form where the two membranes of the envelope fuse. They allow RNA and proteins to move in both directions.\n(3) Wrong: mature sieve tube cells and the erythrocytes of most mammals lack a nucleus.\n(4) Wrong: the outer nuclear membrane is continuous with the ER, not the Golgi, and it bears ribosomes.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-067","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":67,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following membrane-bound organelles is NOT included in the endomembrane system, because its functions are not coordinated with those of the ER and Golgi complex?","image":"","options":["Lysosome","Vacuole","Peroxisome","Golgi complex"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT puts the ER, Golgi complex, lysosomes and vacuoles into the endomembrane system because their functions are coordinated.\nMitochondria, chloroplasts and peroxisomes are membrane-bound, but they work independently of the ER and Golgi, so they are not included.\nLysosomes are tempting to rule out, but they are packaged by the Golgi complex and are part of the system. The vacuole and the Golgi complex are also members.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-068","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":68,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Select one which is not true for ribosome","image":"","options":["Made of two sub-units","Form polysome","May attach to mRNA","Have no role in protein synthesis"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(1) True: A ribosome has a large and a small subunit (60S + 40S in eukaryotes, 50S + 30S in prokaryotes).\n(2) True: Several ribosomes on one mRNA form a polysome.\n(3) True: Ribosomes bind mRNA during translation.\n(4) Not true: Ribosomes are the site of protein synthesis.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 8 (MCQ Q2)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-069","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":69,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about the axoneme of a eukaryotic cilium or flagellum are correct?\n(i) It has nine doublets of radially arranged peripheral microtubules.\n(ii) It has a pair of centrally located microtubules connected by bridges.\n(iii) The central sheath is connected to one tubule of each peripheral doublet by a radial spoke.\n(iv) The peripheral microtubules are present as triplets linked to a central hub.\n(v) The peripheral doublets are interconnected by linkers.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: there are nine peripheral doublets of microtubules arranged radially.\n(ii) Correct: there is a central pair of microtubules joined by bridges and enclosed in a central sheath.\n(iii) Correct: radial spokes connect the central sheath to one tubule of each peripheral doublet.\n(iv) Incorrect: peripheral triplets linked to a central hub describe the centriole or basal body, not the 9+2 axoneme.\n(v) Correct: linkers interconnect the peripheral doublets.\nSo 4 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-070","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":70,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"A piece of plant tissue is treated with a reagent that selectively dissolves calcium pectate and leaves cellulose untouched. What is the most likely immediate result?","image":"","options":["Individual cells lose their shape and burst, because the cellulose wall is digested","Neighbouring cells separate from one another, but each cell keeps its own wall","The plasma membrane of each cell is dissolved, releasing the cytoplasm","Chloroplast grana break down, because their thylakoids are made of pectin"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The middle lamella is mainly calcium pectate, and it glues neighbouring cells together.\nDissolving calcium pectate destroys this cement, so adjacent cells separate.\nEach cell's cellulose-rich wall is untouched, so the cells keep their shape and do not burst. This rules out option (1).\nPlasma membranes are lipid–protein structures, and thylakoids are membranes, not pectin. So options (3) and (4) are wrong.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-071","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":71,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"A protein meant for secretion is made on ribosomes attached to the ER and then modified before it leaves the cell. Which sequence correctly traces its path?","image":"","options":["SER → trans face of Golgi → cisternae → cis face → secretory vesicle","RER → vesicle → trans face of Golgi → cisternae → cis face → secretory vesicle","RER → vesicle → cis face of Golgi → cisternae → trans face → secretory vesicle","Nucleolus → RER → lysosome → cis face of Golgi → secretory vesicle"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Secretory proteins are made on ribosomes attached to the rough ER (RER). The SER has no ribosomes, so it cannot be the starting point.\nThe proteins travel in vesicles that fuse with the convex cis (forming) face of the Golgi.\nThey are modified in the cisternae and leave from the concave trans (maturing) face in secretory vesicles.\nOption (2) reverses the cis and trans faces. Option (4) wrongly sends the protein through the nucleolus and lysosome.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-072","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":72,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (type of chromosome) with List-II (position of centromere):\nList-I\nA. Metacentric\nB. Sub-metacentric\nC. Acrocentric\nD. Telocentric\nList-II\nI. Terminal centromere\nII. Centromere close to one end, giving one extremely short and one very long arm\nIII. Centromere in the middle, giving two equal arms\nIV. Centromere slightly away from the middle, giving one shorter and one longer arm","image":"","options":["A-III, B-II, C-IV, D-I","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A. Metacentric: centromere in the middle, two equal arms → III.\nB. Sub-metacentric: centromere slightly away from the middle, one shorter and one longer arm → IV.\nC. Acrocentric: centromere close to one end, one extremely short and one very long arm → II.\nD. Telocentric: terminal centromere → I.\nTrap: acrocentric means near the end, while telocentric means at the end.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-073","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":73,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following statements is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["The endoplasmic reticulum divides the intracellular space into luminal and extra-luminal (cytoplasmic) compartments.","Rough ER is continuous with the outer membrane of the nucleus.","The cis and trans faces of the Golgi apparatus are entirely different but interconnected.","Mitochondria and chloroplasts are excluded from the endomembrane system because they are not bounded by membranes."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: the ER divides the intracellular space into luminal (inside the ER) and extra-luminal (cytoplasmic) compartments.\n(2) Correct: the RER is continuous with the outer nuclear membrane.\n(3) Correct: the cis and trans faces of the Golgi are entirely different but interconnected.\n(4) Incorrect: mitochondria and chloroplasts are double-membrane-bound. They are excluded from the endomembrane system because their functions are not coordinated with the ER, Golgi, lysosomes and vacuoles, not because they lack membranes.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-074","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":74,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following cell components are considered part of the endomembrane system?\nGolgi complex, mitochondrion, lysosome, peroxisome, smooth endoplasmic reticulum, vacuole, chloroplast, ribosome","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The endomembrane system is the group of membranous organelles whose functions are coordinated: ER, Golgi complex, lysosomes and vacuoles.\nFrom the list, the members are the Golgi complex, lysosome, smooth ER and vacuole, which makes 4.\nThe mitochondrion, chloroplast and peroxisome are excluded. They are membrane-bound, but their functions are not coordinated with the ER and Golgi.\nThe ribosome is excluded because it has no membrane at all.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-075","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":75,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about the Golgi apparatus are correct?\n(i) Densely stained reticular structures near the nucleus were first observed by Camillo Golgi in 1898.\n(ii) Its cisternae are flat, disc-shaped sacs of about 0.5 µm to 1.0 µm diameter.\n(iii) The cis face is concave and is called the maturing face.\n(iv) The cis and trans faces are entirely different but interconnected.\n(v) Proteins synthesised on ER-bound ribosomes are modified in Golgi cisternae before release from the trans face.","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: Camillo Golgi first observed these densely stained reticular structures near the nucleus in 1898.\n(ii) Correct: the cisternae are flat, disc-shaped sacs about 0.5–1.0 µm in diameter.\n(iii) Incorrect: the cis face is convex and is the forming face. The concave trans face is the maturing face.\n(iv) Correct: the two faces are entirely different but interconnected.\n(v) Correct: proteins made on ER-bound ribosomes are modified in the cisternae and released from the trans face.\nFour statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-076","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":76,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"What is a tonoplast?","image":"","options":["Outer membrane of mitochondria","Inner membrane of chloroplast","Membrane boundary of the vacuole of plant cells","Cell membrane of a plant cell"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Key fact: The tonoplast is the single membrane around the plant cell vacuole. It actively transports ions and other materials into the vacuole against a concentration gradient.\nWhy (3) fits: The tonoplast is the vacuolar membrane.\n(4) fails: The plant cell membrane is the plasmalemma.\n(1) and (2) fail: Tonoplast is not a name for any mitochondrial or chloroplast membrane.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 8 (MCQ Q9)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-077","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":77,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In prokaryotes, the genetic material is basically naked and is not enveloped by a nuclear membrane.\nStatement II: Plasmid DNA is used to monitor bacterial transformation with foreign DNA.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct: Prokaryotes lack a well-defined nucleus. Their genomic DNA is a single circular chromosome that lies naked in the cytoplasm, with no nuclear membrane around it.\nStatement II is correct: NCERT states that plasmid DNA is used to monitor bacterial transformation with foreign DNA. Plasmids also give traits such as antibiotic resistance.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-078","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":78,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"The nucleus was first described as a cell organelle in 1831 by one scientist. A second scientist later named the nuclear material that stains with basic dyes 'chromatin'. Who were these two scientists, in that order?","image":"","options":["Antonie von Leeuwenhoek; Rudolf Virchow","Robert Brown; Flemming","Robert Brown; Camillo Golgi","Matthias Schleiden; Flemming"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Robert Brown first described the nucleus as a cell organelle in 1831.\nFlemming later named the nuclear material that stains with basic dyes 'chromatin'.\nThe other names do not fit. Leeuwenhoek first saw a live cell, Virchow proposed that cells arise from pre-existing cells, Golgi described the Golgi apparatus, and Schleiden worked on plant cells for the cell theory.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-079","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":79,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Given below are two statements:\nStatement I: Vesicles carrying materials from the ER fuse with the concave trans face of the Golgi apparatus and move towards the convex cis face.\nStatement II: The Golgi apparatus is an important site for the formation of glycoproteins and glycolipids.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I is incorrect: ER vesicles fuse with the convex cis (forming) face of the Golgi and move towards the concave trans (maturing) face. The statement reverses the faces.\nStatement II is correct: the Golgi apparatus is an important site for the formation of glycoproteins and glycolipids.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-080","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":80,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about prokaryotic cells is incorrect?","image":"","options":["Mesosomes help in cell wall formation, DNA replication and distribution to daughter cells","Inclusion bodies such as glycogen and phosphate granules are enclosed by a single membrane","Plasmid DNA can confer resistance to antibiotics","Prokaryotic ribosomes are associated with the plasma membrane and measure about 15 nm by 20 nm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: NCERT states that mesosomes help in cell wall formation, DNA replication and distribution, respiration and secretion.\n(2) Incorrect: inclusion bodies such as phosphate granules and glycogen granules are not bound by any membrane. They lie free in the cytoplasm.\n(3) Correct: plasmids carry extra genes, such as genes for antibiotic resistance.\n(4) Correct: prokaryotic ribosomes are associated with the plasma membrane and measure about 15 nm × 20 nm.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-081","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":81,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"As a plant cell matures, its secondary wall is deposited:","image":"","options":["On the inner side of the primary wall, towards the plasma membrane","On the outer side of the middle lamella","Between the primary wall and the middle lamella","Inside the cytoplasm as a separate layer around the vacuole"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT fact: as the cell matures, the secondary wall forms on the inner side of the primary wall, facing the plasma membrane.\nSo the primary wall stays outermost, next to the middle lamella.\nOption (3), between the primary wall and the middle lamella, is the tempting trap. That position is wrong because the primary walls of neighbouring cells are cemented directly by the middle lamella.\nThe wall is never formed inside the cytoplasm.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-082","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":82,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Rough endoplasmic reticulum is extensive in cells that actively synthesise and secrete proteins.\nReason (R): Rough endoplasmic reticulum bears ribosomes, the sites of protein synthesis, on its outer surface.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. Cells that actively make and secrete proteins have extensive RER.\nR is true. The RER bears ribosomes on its outer surface, and ribosomes are the sites of protein synthesis.\nBecause the ribosomes make proteins, more RER means a greater capacity to synthesise and secrete proteins.\nSo R directly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-083","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":83,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"In some bacteria the glycocalyx is thick and tough rather than a loose sheath. What is this thick, tough glycocalyx called?","image":"","options":["Slime layer","Mesosome","Capsule","Chromatophore"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The glycocalyx varies in composition and thickness among bacteria.\nA loose sheath is called the slime layer. A thick, tough glycocalyx is called a capsule.\nMesosomes are infoldings of the plasma membrane. Chromatophores are membranous pigment-containing extensions found in some photosynthetic bacteria.\nSo the thick, tough glycocalyx is the capsule.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-084","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":84,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following locations contain 70S ribosomes?\nCytoplasm of a bacterium, Mitochondrial matrix of a liver cell, Stroma of a chloroplast, Rough ER of a pancreatic cell, Cytoplasm of a cyanobacterium, Free cytoplasm of a yeast cell","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"70S ribosomes occur in prokaryotes and inside mitochondria and chloroplasts.\nBacterial cytoplasm has 70S ribosomes.\nThe mitochondrial matrix has 70S ribosomes.\nThe chloroplast stroma has 70S ribosomes.\nCyanobacterial cytoplasm has 70S ribosomes, because cyanobacteria are prokaryotes.\nRibosomes on the rough ER and in yeast cytoplasm are eukaryotic 80S ribosomes.\nSo there are 4 locations.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-085","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":85,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are considered part of the endomembrane system?\nEndoplasmic reticulum, Golgi complex, Mitochondria, Lysosomes, Chloroplast, Vacuoles, Peroxisomes","image":"","options":["Three","Four","Five","Seven"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT defines the endomembrane system as the organelles whose functions are coordinated.\nIncluded: endoplasmic reticulum, Golgi complex, lysosomes and vacuoles. That makes 4.\nExcluded: mitochondria, chloroplasts and peroxisomes. They are membrane-bound, but their functions are not coordinated with the organelles above.\nSo four of the listed organelles belong to the endomembrane system.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-086","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":86,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"In some bacteria the outermost layer of the cell envelope, the glycocalyx, is thick and tough. This form of glycocalyx is called the:","image":"","options":["Slime layer","Capsule","Mesosome","Middle lamella"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Key fact: The glycocalyx varies among bacteria in composition and thickness.\nWhen it is thick and tough, it is called a capsule.\nTempting wrong option: A slime layer is a loose sheath, not a thick, tough one.\nA mesosome is a plasma membrane infolding, and the middle lamella cements adjacent plant cells together.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-087","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":87,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The content of the nucleolus is continuous with the rest of the nucleoplasm.\nReason (R): The nucleolus is not a membrane-bound structure.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: NCERT states that the contents of the nucleolus are continuous with the rest of the nucleoplasm.\nR is true: the nucleolus is a spherical body with no membrane around it.\nR explains A. Because no membrane separates the nucleolus from the nucleoplasm, its contents merge directly with it.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-088","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":88,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following is not true of a eukaryotic cell?","image":"","options":["Cell wall is made up of peptidoglycans","80S type of ribosomes are present in the cytoplasm","Mitochondria contain circular DNA","Membrane bound organelles are present"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(1) Not true: peptidoglycan cell walls are found in bacteria (prokaryotes). Eukaryotic cell walls are made of cellulose in plants and chitin in fungi.\n(2) True: the eukaryotic cytoplasm has 80S ribosomes.\n(3) True: mitochondria contain their own circular DNA and 70S ribosomes.\n(4) True: membrane-bound organelles such as the nucleus, ER and Golgi apparatus are a hallmark of eukaryotes.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 8 (MCQ Q10)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-089","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":89,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"The name 'chromatin' was given to the material of the nucleus that is stained by basic dyes. Who gave it this name?","image":"","options":["Robert Brown","Flemming","Camillo Golgi","Rudolf Virchow"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Flemming named the nuclear material that is stained by basic dyes 'chromatin'.\nRobert Brown is the tempting option, but he described the nucleus itself in 1831, not chromatin.\nCamillo Golgi discovered the Golgi apparatus.\nRudolf Virchow proposed that new cells arise from pre-existing cells (Omnis cellula-e cellula).\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-090","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":90,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"A cell is treated with a poison that completely stops ATP production, while the membrane itself stays intact. Which of the following processes would be directly stopped?","image":"","options":["Diffusion of a neutral solute along its concentration gradient","Osmotic movement of water from higher to lower water concentration","Carrier protein-mediated passage of a polar molecule down its concentration gradient","Pumping of Na$^+$ ions from a region of lower to higher concentration"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Key idea: only transport against a concentration gradient needs ATP (active transport).\nOptions (1) and (2) are passive. Diffusion of a neutral solute and osmosis both go down a gradient, so they continue without ATP.\nOption (3) is the tempting distractor because a carrier protein is involved. However, the movement is down the gradient, so it is passive and continues.\nOption (4) pumps Na$^+$ from lower to higher concentration. This is active transport, so it stops once ATP runs out.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-091","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":91,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Plastid differs from mitochondria on the basis of one of the following features. Mark the right answer.","image":"","options":["Presence of two layers of membrane","Presence of ribosome","Presence of thylakoids","Presence of DNA"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Plastids and mitochondria are both semi-autonomous organelles. Each has a double membrane, its own circular DNA and 70S ribosomes, so options (1), (2) and (4) are features they share.\nThylakoids are flattened membranous sacs, stacked into grana, in the chloroplast stroma. They carry the photosynthetic pigments.\nMitochondria have cristae instead, never thylakoids. So thylakoids are the distinguishing feature.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 8 (MCQ Q12)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-092","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":92,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Plasmodesmata are best described as:","image":"","options":["Infoldings of the plasma membrane that increase its surface area","Pores in the nuclear envelope that allow RNA to move out","Connections through the cell wall and middle lamella that link the cytoplasm of neighbouring cells","Thickenings of the secondary wall that give mechanical strength"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT fact: plasmodesmata are cytoplasmic connections that pass through the cell wall and middle lamella, linking neighbouring plant cells.\nOption (3) fits this description.\nOption (1) describes mesosomes, which are membrane infoldings in prokaryotes.\nOption (2) describes nuclear pores.\nOption (4) describes secondary wall thickenings, which are not channels between cells.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-093","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":93,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A prokaryotic ribosome made of a 50S and a 30S subunit is called a 70S ribosome and not an 80S ribosome.\nReason (R): 'S' is the Svedberg unit of sedimentation coefficient, which is indirectly a measure of density and size, so subunit S values do not simply add up.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: a prokaryotic ribosome with 50S and 30S subunits is called a 70S ribosome.\nR is true: S is the Svedberg unit of sedimentation coefficient. It indirectly measures density and size.\nSedimentation rate depends on the shape and size of the whole particle, so subunit S values are not additive.\nThis is exactly why $50S + 30S$ gives 70S and not 80S, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-094","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":94,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Chromoplasts impart yellow, orange or red colour to some parts of a plant.\nReason (R): Chromoplasts contain water-soluble pigments such as chlorophyll dissolved in the stroma.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. Chromoplasts give yellow, orange or red colours to plant parts such as fruits and petals.\nR is false. These colours come from fat-soluble carotenoid pigments such as carotene and xanthophylls. Chromoplasts do not contain chlorophyll, and chlorophyll is not water-soluble.\nSo A is true but R is false.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-095","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":95,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements is incorrect?","image":"","options":["Cytoplasm is the main arena of cellular activities in both prokaryotic and eukaryotic cells","In a eukaryotic cell, ribosomes occur in the cytoplasm, on rough ER, and inside mitochondria and chloroplasts","The centrosome of animal cells, which helps in cell division, is a single-membrane bound organelle","Nerve cells are some of the longest cells"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct. Cytoplasm is the main arena of cellular activities in both prokaryotic and eukaryotic cells.\n(2) Correct. In eukaryotes, ribosomes occur free in the cytoplasm, on rough ER, and inside mitochondria and chloroplasts.\n(3) Incorrect. The centrosome, made of two centrioles, is a non-membrane bound organelle, not a single-membrane bound one.\n(4) Correct. Nerve cells are among the longest cells.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-096","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":96,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (plastid) with List-II (content):\nList-I\n(a) Amyloplast\n(b) Elaioplast\n(c) Aleuroplast\n(d) Chromoplast\nList-II\n(i) Proteins\n(ii) Fat-soluble carotenoids such as xanthophylls\n(iii) Starch\n(iv) Oils and fats","image":"","options":["a-iii, b-iv, c-i, d-ii","a-iv, b-iii, c-i, d-ii","a-iii, b-i, c-iv, d-ii","a-i, b-iv, c-iii, d-ii"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Amyloplasts store starch, as in potato: a–iii.\n(b) Elaioplasts store oils and fats: b–iv.\n(c) Aleuroplasts store proteins: c–i.\n(d) Chromoplasts contain fat-soluble carotenoid pigments such as carotene and xanthophylls: d–ii.\nWatch out: the common mistake is swapping elaioplasts (oils) and aleuroplasts (proteins).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-097","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":97,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"In Amoeba, the contractile vacuole is mainly important for:","image":"","options":["Digestion of engulfed food particles","Osmoregulation and excretion","Storage of starch","Synthesis of lipids"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"In Amoeba, the contractile vacuole collects excess water and wastes and expels them. Its job is therefore osmoregulation and excretion.\nThe tempting wrong choice is digestion. Engulfed particles are digested in food vacuoles, which are a different structure.\nStoring starch and making lipids are not functions of this vacuole.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-098","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":98,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Given below are two statements about the Golgi apparatus.\nStatement I: The cis face of the Golgi apparatus is concave and is called the maturing face.\nStatement II: The Golgi apparatus is an important site of formation of glycoproteins and glycolipids.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is incorrect. The cis face is convex and is the forming face. The trans face is concave and is the maturing face.\nStatement II is correct. NCERT calls the Golgi apparatus an important site for the formation of glycoproteins and glycolipids.\nSo Statement I is wrong and Statement II is right.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-099","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":99,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"The arrangement of microtubules in the axoneme of a typical eukaryotic cilium or flagellum is described as:","image":"","options":["9 + 0, with nine peripheral triplets","9 + 2, with nine peripheral doublets and a central pair","9 + 2, with nine peripheral triplets and a central pair","9 + 1, with nine peripheral doublets and one central tubule"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The axoneme of a cilium or flagellum has nine radially arranged peripheral doublet microtubules and a pair of central microtubules.\nThis is called the 9+2 array.\nTriplets are found in centrioles and basal bodies, not in the axoneme, so option (3) is wrong.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-100","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":100,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"The hydrolytic enzymes present in lysosomes are optimally active at:","image":"","options":["Acidic pH","Neutral pH","Alkaline pH","Any pH between 2 and 12"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Lysosomes contain hydrolytic enzymes such as lipases, proteases and carbohydrases.\nNCERT states that these enzymes are optimally active at acidic pH.\nNeutral pH is the tempting wrong choice because it matches the cytoplasm. However, the lysosome lumen is kept acidic, which helps protect the cell if the enzymes leak out.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-101","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":101,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"A colourless plastid isolated from the seed of an oil-yielding plant is found to store oils and fats. This plastid is a/an","image":"","options":["amyloplast","aleuroplast","elaioplast","chromoplast"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Colourless plastids are leucoplasts. They are classified by the food they store.\nAmyloplasts store starch, aleuroplasts store proteins and elaioplasts store oils and fats.\nAn oil-storing colourless plastid in an oilseed is therefore an elaioplast.\nChromoplasts are excluded because they are coloured, containing carotenoid pigments.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-102","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":102,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"New cells generate from","image":"","options":["bacterial fermentation","regeneration of old cells","pre-existing cells","abiotic materials"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Key fact: Rudolf Virchow (1855) proposed 'Omnis cellula-e cellula', meaning all cells arise from pre-existing cells. This principle completed the cell theory.\nWhy the other options fail:\n- Abiotic materials: this would be spontaneous generation, which is rejected.\n- Fermentation: it is a metabolic process, not a source of new cells.\n- 'Regeneration of old cells': this is not how new cells form. Cells arise by division of existing cells.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 (Exercise Q2)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-103","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":103,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"The layer that cements neighbouring plant cells together is chiefly made of which substance?","image":"","options":["Cellulose","Calcium pectate","Calcium carbonate","Hemicellulose"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT fact: the middle lamella is the layer that glues neighbouring plant cells together.\nIt is made mainly of calcium pectate, which fits option (2).\nCellulose and hemicellulose are components of the cell wall itself, not the cementing layer.\nCalcium carbonate is a mineral found in some algal cell walls.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-104","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":104,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following is NOT a component of the cytoskeleton of a eukaryotic cell?","image":"","options":["Microtubules","Microfilaments","Intermediate filaments","Cisternae"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"The cytoskeleton is a network of proteinaceous filaments: microtubules, microfilaments and intermediate filaments.\nIt provides mechanical support, helps in motility and maintains cell shape.\nCisternae are flattened membranous sacs of the ER and Golgi apparatus. They are part of the endomembrane system, not the cytoskeleton.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-105","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":105,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\n(a) Filament\n(b) Pili\n(c) Fimbriae\n(d) Polysome\nList-II\n(i) Elongated tubular structures made of a special protein\n(ii) Several ribosomes attached to a single mRNA\n(iii) Longest portion of the bacterial flagellum\n(iv) Small bristle-like fibres sprouting out of the cell","image":"","options":["a-iii, b-i, c-iv, d-ii","a-iii, b-iv, c-i, d-ii","a-i, b-iii, c-iv, d-ii","a-iii, b-i, c-ii, d-iv"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Filament: the longest portion of the bacterial flagellum, so (iii).\n(b) Pili: elongated tubular structures made of a special protein, so (i).\n(c) Fimbriae: small bristle-like fibres sprouting out of the cell, so (iv).\n(d) Polysome: several ribosomes attached to a single mRNA, so (ii).\nThe usual trap is swapping pili and fimbriae. Pili are tubular, while fimbriae are bristle-like.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-106","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":106,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"While studying animal cells, Theodore Schwann reported a thin outer layer around the cells. This layer is known today as the:","image":"","options":["Cell wall","Middle lamella","Plasma membrane","Nuclear envelope"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Key fact: Schwann studied animal cells and reported a thin outer layer around them.\nToday this layer is called the plasma membrane.\nThe cell wall is the tempting option, but it is wrong. Schwann himself concluded that the cell wall is unique to plant cells, so it cannot be the layer he saw in animal cells.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-107","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":107,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Within a chloroplast, chlorophyll pigments are present in the","image":"","options":["stroma","outer membrane","inner membrane","thylakoids"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"In a chloroplast, the thylakoids are flattened membranous sacs, stacked as grana. Chlorophyll pigments are embedded in the thylakoid membranes.\nThe stroma is the fluid matrix. It holds enzymes, DNA and ribosomes but not the pigments.\nThe outer and inner envelope membranes do not contain chlorophyll.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-108","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":108,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Pili are elongated tubular surface structures of bacteria made of a special protein.\nStatement II: Pili, like flagella, are the organs of motility in bacteria.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Pili are elongated tubular surface structures made of a special protein.\nStatement II is incorrect. Pili and fimbriae play no role in motility. Only flagella help bacteria move.\nPili help in attachment and in conjugation instead.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-109","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":109,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Among prokaryotes, mycoplasma is the exception in lacking a cell wall around the cell membrane.\nStatement II: The two centrioles of a centrosome lie parallel to each other.\nChoose the correct option:","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I: Correct. NCERT states that all prokaryotes have a cell wall surrounding the cell membrane, except mycoplasma.\nStatement II: Incorrect. The two cylindrical centrioles of a centrosome lie perpendicular to each other, not parallel.\nSo Statement I is correct and Statement II is incorrect.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-110","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":110,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"The 70S ribosome of a prokaryotic cell is formed by the association of which two subunits?","image":"","options":["60S and 40S","50S and 30S","40S and 30S","50S and 20S"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT fact: prokaryotic ribosomes are 70S and are made of two subunits.\nThe large subunit is 50S and the small subunit is 30S.\nThe tempting wrong option is 60S + 40S. That pair forms the eukaryotic 80S ribosome, not the prokaryotic one.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-111","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":111,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In plant cells, the concentration of many ions is significantly higher inside the vacuole than in the cytoplasm.\nStatement II: The plant vacuole is bounded by a double membrane that allows ions to diffuse freely into it.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Many ions are much more concentrated in the vacuole than in the cytoplasm.\nStatement II is incorrect, for two reasons:\n- The vacuole is bounded by a single membrane, the tonoplast, not a double membrane.\n- Ions do not diffuse freely in. The tonoplast actively transports them against their concentration gradient.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-112","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":112,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following locations would contain 70S ribosomes?\nCytoplasm of a bacterium; mitochondrial matrix; chloroplast stroma; surface of rough ER in a liver cell; cytoplasm of a cyanobacterium; free cytoplasmic ribosomes of an onion root cell","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"70S ribosomes occur in prokaryotes and inside mitochondria and chloroplasts. Eukaryotic cytoplasmic and RER-bound ribosomes are 80S.\nLocations with 70S: bacterial cytoplasm ✓, mitochondrial matrix ✓, chloroplast stroma ✓, cyanobacterial cytoplasm ✓.\nLocations with 80S: rough ER of a liver cell ✗, free ribosomes of an onion root cell ✗.\nTotal = 4 locations.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-113","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":113,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"A student counts the individual microtubules seen in a transverse section of (P) the axoneme of a typical eukaryotic flagellum and (Q) a centriole. The correct counts for P and Q respectively are:","image":"","options":["11 and 9","18 and 27","20 and 27","20 and 9"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"P (axoneme): there are 9 peripheral doublets, giving $9 \\times 2 = 18$ microtubules. Add the 2 central singlets: $18 + 2 = 20$.\nQ (centriole): there are 9 peripheral triplets, giving $9 \\times 3 = 27$ microtubules. A centriole has no central pair.\nTraps to avoid: answering 11 means counting doublets as single units, and answering 18 means forgetting the central pair.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-114","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":114,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Which scientist first saw and described a live cell?","image":"","options":["Robert Brown","Antonie Von Leeuwenhoek","Theodore Schwann","Rudolf Virchow"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Key NCERT fact: Antonie Von Leeuwenhoek was the first to see and describe a live cell.\nRobert Brown is the tempting option, but he came later and discovered the nucleus.\nSchwann and Virchow contributed to the cell theory. Neither was the first to observe a living cell.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-115","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":115,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements is true for a secretory cell?","image":"","options":["Golgi apparatus is absent","Rough Endoplasmic Reticulum (RER) is easily observed in the cell","Only Smooth Endoplasmic Reticulum (SER) is present","Secretory granules are formed in nucleus."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Key fact: Secretory cells make large amounts of protein. Ribosome-studded RER is therefore abundant and easy to see.\nWhy (2) fits: An extensive RER is typical of protein-secreting cells.\n(1) fails: The Golgi apparatus is well developed, because it packages the secretions.\n(3) fails: RER is present, not only SER.\n(4) fails: Secretory granules form from the Golgi, not the nucleus.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 8 (MCQ Q8)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-116","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":116,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following is not correct?","image":"","options":["Robert Brown discovered the cell.","Schleiden and Schwann formulated the cell theory.","Virchow explained that cells are formed from pre-existing cells.","A unicellular organism carries out its life activities within a single cell."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(1) Incorrect: Robert Hooke first observed cells, in cork, in 1665. Robert Brown later discovered the nucleus (1831).\n(2) Correct: Schleiden (1838) and Schwann (1839) together formulated the cell theory.\n(3) Correct: Virchow (1855) stated that new cells arise from pre-existing cells.\n(4) Correct: a unicellular organism performs all its life activities within one cell.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 (Exercise Q1)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-117","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":117,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In the plasma membrane, phospholipids are arranged so that their polar heads face the outer sides and their hydrophobic tails face the inner part of the bilayer.\nStatement II: Membrane proteins are classified as integral and peripheral on the basis of their role in active transport.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct: polar heads face outward towards water, and hydrophobic tails face the interior of the bilayer.\nStatement II is incorrect: proteins are classified as integral or peripheral by how easily they can be extracted, not by their role in transport.\nPeripheral proteins lie on the surface, while integral proteins are partly or totally buried in the bilayer.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-118","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":118,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the nuclear envelope is incorrect?","image":"","options":["Nuclear pores are formed by fusion of the two membranes of the envelope","RNA and protein molecules move through nuclear pores in both directions","The inner nuclear membrane bears ribosomes and is continuous with the Golgi apparatus","The outer nuclear membrane is usually continuous with the endoplasmic reticulum"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: at nuclear pores the inner and outer membranes fuse.\n(2) Correct: RNA and proteins move in both directions through the pores.\n(3) Incorrect: the outer membrane, not the inner one, bears ribosomes and is continuous with the ER. Neither membrane is described as continuous with the Golgi apparatus.\n(4) Correct: the outer membrane usually remains continuous with the ER.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-119","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":119,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["The secondary cell wall is formed on the inner side of the primary wall, towards the plasma membrane","Of the two chloroplast membranes, the inner membrane is relatively less permeable","Vesicles carrying material from the ER fuse with the concave trans face of the Golgi apparatus","The tonoplast transports ions into the vacuole against the concentration gradient, so their concentration in the vacuole exceeds that in the cytoplasm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: the secondary wall is laid down inside the primary wall, towards the plasma membrane.\n(2) Correct: of the two chloroplast membranes, the inner one is relatively less permeable.\n(3) Incorrect: ER-derived vesicles fuse with the convex cis (forming) face of the Golgi. Materials then move to the concave trans (maturing) face and leave from there.\n(4) Correct: the tonoplast pumps ions into the vacuole against the concentration gradient, so the vacuole holds a higher concentration than the cytoplasm.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-120","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":120,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Mesosomes help in DNA replication and in the distribution of DNA to daughter cells in bacteria.\nReason (R): Mesosomes are formed by extensions of the bacterial cell wall into the cytoplasm.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true: Mesosomes help in DNA replication and in distributing DNA to daughter cells. They also help in respiration, secretion and cell wall formation.\nR is false: Mesosomes are formed by extensions of the plasma membrane into the cell, not the cell wall.\nSo A is true and R is false.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-121","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":121,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"A described diagram of a cell structure, seen in transverse section, shows the following: nine evenly spaced peripheral fibrils of tubulin, each a triplet, with adjacent triplets linked. A central proteinaceous hub is joined to the peripheral tubules by radial spokes, giving a cartwheel appearance. Two such cylinders lie perpendicular to each other. The structure is:","image":"","options":["Axoneme of a cilium","Centriole","Basal body of a bacterial flagellum","Microfilament bundle of the cytoskeleton"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A centriole is built of nine evenly spaced peripheral triplet fibrils of tubulin, with adjacent triplets linked.\nIts central proteinaceous hub is joined to the peripheral triplets by radial spokes, which gives the cartwheel look. A centrosome has two centrioles lying perpendicular to each other.\nThe tempting option, the axoneme of a cilium, has a 9+2 pattern of doublets with a central pair, not triplets.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-122","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":122,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the bacterial cell envelope is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Although each envelope layer has a distinct function, the layers act together as a single protective unit","The division of bacteria into Gram positive and Gram negative is based on differences in the cell envelope and response to Gram's staining","The bacterial plasma membrane is structurally similar to that of eukaryotes","The glycocalyx is uniform in composition and thickness in all bacteria"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: Each layer has a distinct function, but together they act as a single protective unit.\n(2) Correct: The Gram positive and Gram negative grouping is based on envelope differences and response to Gram's staining.\n(3) Correct: The bacterial plasma membrane is structurally similar to that of eukaryotes.\n(4) Incorrect: The glycocalyx differs in composition and thickness among bacteria. It can be a loose slime layer or a thick, tough capsule.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-123","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":123,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are functions attributed to mesosomes in prokaryotic cells?\n(i) Cell wall formation\n(ii) DNA replication\n(iii) Housing photosynthetic pigments\n(iv) Respiration\n(v) Secretion processes\n(vi) Attachment of bacteria to host tissues","image":"","options":["Three","Four","Five","Six"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Cell wall formation: correct, a mesosome function.\n(ii) DNA replication: correct.\n(iii) Housing photosynthetic pigments: incorrect, this is done by chromatophores.\n(iv) Respiration: correct.\n(v) Secretion processes: correct.\n(vi) Attachment to host tissues: incorrect, this is done by fimbriae.\nSo four items (i, ii, iv, v) are mesosome functions.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-124","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":124,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Of the two membranes of a chloroplast, the inner membrane is relatively less permeable.\nStatement II: The space enclosed by the thylakoid membrane is called the stroma.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Of the two chloroplast membranes, NCERT states that the inner membrane is relatively less permeable.\nStatement II is incorrect. The space enclosed by the thylakoid membrane is the lumen. The stroma is the space bounded by the inner membrane of the chloroplast.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-125","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":125,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"A sectional diagram of a chloroplast shows three labelled parts:\nX: stacks of flattened membranous sacs arranged like piles of coins\nY: flat tubules linking sacs of different stacks\nZ: the matrix enclosed by the inner membrane\nWhich of the following statements about this diagram is correct?","image":"","options":["Chlorophyll pigments are located in Z","X are grana, the site of light reactions, and Z contains enzymes for synthesis of carbohydrates and proteins","Y are cristae that increase the surface area for photosynthesis","The lumen is the space between the outer and the inner chloroplast membranes"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"X: stacks of thylakoids arranged like piles of coins are grana. They carry out the light reactions.\nY: flat tubules linking thylakoids of different grana are stroma lamellae, not cristae. Cristae belong to mitochondria. So (3) is wrong.\nZ: the matrix inside the inner membrane is the stroma. It has enzymes for carbohydrate and protein synthesis.\n(1) is wrong because chlorophyll lies in the thylakoid membranes, not in the stroma.\n(4) is wrong because the lumen is the space enclosed by the thylakoid membrane.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-126","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":126,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"In some prokaryotes such as cyanobacteria, pigments are held in membranous extensions of the plasma membrane into the cytoplasm. These structures are called:","image":"","options":["Thylakoids of chloroplasts","Chromatophores","Plasmids","Inclusion bodies"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT fact: in some prokaryotes such as cyanobacteria, the plasma membrane extends into the cytoplasm as membranous structures that hold pigments. These are called chromatophores.\nThe keyed option fits because chromatophores are exactly these pigment-bearing membrane extensions.\nThylakoids of chloroplasts are the tempting wrong option, but prokaryotes have no chloroplasts.\nPlasmids are extra-chromosomal DNA, not membranes.\nInclusion bodies store reserve material and are not bound by any membrane.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-127","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":127,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"A chromosome has its centromere situated close to one end, forming one extremely short arm and one very long arm. This chromosome is classified as:","image":"","options":["Metacentric","Sub-metacentric","Telocentric","Acrocentric"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"A centromere close to one end gives one extremely short arm and one very long arm. This defines an acrocentric chromosome.\nThe most tempting wrong option is telocentric, but a telocentric chromosome has a strictly terminal centromere.\nA metacentric chromosome has the centromere in the middle, and a sub-metacentric one has it only slightly away from the middle.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-128","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":128,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Every chromosome possesses a secondary constriction that gives it a satellite.\nStatement II: The centromere, or primary constriction, holds the two chromatids of a chromosome, and kinetochores lie on its sides.\nChoose the correct option:","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is incorrect. Only a few chromosomes have a non-staining secondary constriction that gives a satellite.\nStatement II is correct. Every chromosome has a primary constriction, the centromere. It holds the two chromatids together, and disc-shaped kinetochores lie on its sides.\nTrap: the primary constriction is universal, but the secondary constriction is occasional.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-129","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":129,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about the plasma membrane are correct?\n(i) The quasi-fluid nature of lipids enables lateral movement of proteins within the bilayer.\n(ii) Membrane fluidity is important for endocytosis, secretion and cell division.\n(iii) The Na$^+$/K$^+$ pump moves ions along their concentration gradient without using ATP.\n(iv) Before the electron microscope era, chemical studies on human RBCs helped scientists deduce the possible structure of the membrane.\n(v) The ratio of protein to lipid is the same in the membranes of all cell types.","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: the quasi-fluid lipids allow proteins to move laterally within the bilayer.\n(ii) Correct: fluidity is important for endocytosis, secretion and cell division.\n(iii) Incorrect: the Na$^+$/K$^+$ pump is active transport. It uses ATP and moves ions against their gradient.\n(iv) Correct: chemical studies on human RBC membranes helped deduce membrane structure before electron microscopy.\n(v) Incorrect: the protein-to-lipid ratio varies between cell types.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-130","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":130,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are functions of the plant cell wall?\n(i) Giving shape to the cell\n(ii) Protection from mechanical damage\n(iii) Protection from infection\n(iv) Helping in cell-to-cell interaction\n(v) Acting as a barrier to undesirable macromolecules\n(vi) Synthesis of ATP\n(vii) Synthesis of steroidal hormones","image":"","options":["3","4","5","7"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: the wall gives shape to the cell.\n(ii), (iii) Correct: it protects against mechanical damage and infection.\n(iv) Correct: it helps in cell-to-cell interaction.\n(v) Correct: it acts as a barrier to undesirable macromolecules.\n(vi) Incorrect: ATP is made mainly in mitochondria (and in chloroplasts).\n(vii) Incorrect: steroidal hormones are made in the SER of animal cells.\nTotal = 5 functions.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-131","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":131,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about inclusion bodies of prokaryotic cells are correct?\n(i) They are bounded by a single membrane.\n(ii) They lie free in the cytoplasm.\n(iii) They store reserve material.\n(iv) Glycogen granules and cyanophycean granules are examples.\n(v) Gas vacuoles occur in all bacteria.","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Incorrect: inclusion bodies are not bounded by any membrane.\n(ii) Correct: they lie free in the cytoplasm.\n(iii) Correct: they store reserve material.\n(iv) Correct: phosphate, cyanophycean and glycogen granules are examples.\n(v) Incorrect: gas vacuoles occur only in blue-green algae and in purple and green photosynthetic bacteria, not in all bacteria.\nSo three statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-132","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":132,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\nA. Ribosome in the cytoplasm of a eukaryotic cell\nB. Larger subunit of a prokaryotic ribosome\nC. Smaller subunit of a eukaryotic ribosome\nD. Ribosome in the mitochondrial matrix\nList-II\n(i) 40S\n(ii) 70S\n(iii) 50S\n(iv) 80S\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iv), B-(i), C-(iii), D-(ii)","A-(ii), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(iv)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(ii), D-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A: The cytoplasmic eukaryotic ribosome is 80S, made of 60S + 40S subunits → (iv).\nB: The larger subunit of the prokaryotic 70S ribosome is 50S → (iii).\nC: The smaller subunit of the eukaryotic ribosome is 40S → (i).\nD: Mitochondrial ribosomes are prokaryote-like 70S → (ii).\nTrap: assigning 80S to mitochondrial ribosomes just because the organelle sits inside a eukaryotic cell.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-133","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":133,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\n(A) Rough endoplasmic reticulum\n(B) Smooth endoplasmic reticulum\n(C) Golgi apparatus\n(D) Vacuole\nList-II\n(i) Bounded by tonoplast; may occupy up to 90% of plant cell volume\n(ii) Important site of formation of glycoproteins and glycolipids\n(iii) Major site of lipid synthesis\n(iv) Continuous with the outer nuclear membrane; abundant in secretory cells\nChoose the correct option:","image":"","options":["A-iv, B-iii, C-ii, D-i","A-iii, B-iv, C-ii, D-i","A-iv, B-ii, C-iii, D-i","A-ii, B-iii, C-iv, D-i"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A–iv: Rough ER is continuous with the outer nuclear membrane and is abundant in cells that make and secrete proteins.\nB–iii: Smooth ER is the major site of lipid synthesis.\nC–ii: The Golgi apparatus is an important site for forming glycoproteins and glycolipids.\nD–i: The vacuole is bounded by the tonoplast and can occupy up to 90% of a plant cell's volume.\nThis gives A-iv, B-iii, C-ii, D-i.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-134","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":134,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"The approximate diameter of a human red blood cell is","image":"","options":["0.7 $\\mu m$","7.0 $\\mu m$","70 $\\mu m$","7.0 mm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT gives the diameter of a human red blood cell as about 7.0 $\\mu m$.\n0.7 $\\mu m$ is ten times too small, close to bacterial or Mycoplasma sizes.\n70 $\\mu m$ is ten times too large.\n7.0 mm uses the wrong unit and is 1000 times too large.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-135","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":135,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following observations, if it were ever genuinely made, would contradict the cell theory as it is understood today?","image":"","options":["A mature mammalian erythrocyte that lacks a nucleus","An organism whose entire body consists of a single cell","An animal cell that has a plasma membrane but no cell wall","Living cells appearing spontaneously from non-living material without any pre-existing cell"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Modern cell theory has two key ideas: all living organisms are made of cells, and all cells arise from pre-existing cells (Virchow, *omnis cellula e cellula*).\n(1), (2) and (3) are all consistent with the theory. It does not require every cell to have a nucleus or a cell wall, and a unicellular organism is still made of a cell.\n(4) Cells arising spontaneously from non-living matter would directly violate 'cells arise from pre-existing cells'.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-136","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":136,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\n(A) Middle lamella\n(B) Algal cell wall\n(C) Primary wall\n(D) Plasmodesmata\nList-II\n(I) Capable of growth in a young cell\n(II) Cytoplasmic connections between adjacent cells\n(III) Mainly calcium pectate\n(IV) Galactans and mannans\nChoose the correct answer:","image":"","options":["A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A–III: The middle lamella is mainly calcium pectate, which cements neighbouring cells.\nB–IV: The algal cell wall contains galactans and mannans, along with cellulose and CaCO$_3$.\nC–I: The primary wall of a young cell is capable of growth.\nD–II: Plasmodesmata are cytoplasmic connections between adjacent cells.\nThis gives A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-137","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":137,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"A unicellular organism is considered a complete living unit because it is capable of:","image":"","options":["Independent existence and performing the essential functions of life","Forming tissues and organs from a single cell","Surviving only when attached to a host cell","Photosynthesis and cell wall formation in all cases"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Key NCERT fact: unicellular organisms are capable of (i) independent existence and (ii) performing all the essential functions of life.\nAnything less than a complete cell cannot ensure independent living, which is why the cell is the basic unit of life.\nForming tissues and organs is a feature of multicellular organisms. Dependence on a host contradicts independent existence.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-138","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":138,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Matthias Schleiden was a German zoologist who reported that animal cells have a thin outer layer.\nStatement II: Based on his studies of plant tissues, Theodore Schwann concluded that the presence of a cell wall is a unique character of plant cells.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is incorrect. Schleiden was a German botanist who studied plants. It was Schwann, a British zoologist, who reported the thin outer layer of animal cells.\nStatement II is correct. Based on his studies of plant tissues, Schwann concluded that the cell wall is unique to plant cells.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-139","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":139,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"In a mesophyll cell of a green leaf, the ribosomes in the chloroplast stroma and the ribosomes free in the cytoplasm are respectively:","image":"","options":["80S and 70S","70S and 80S","70S and 70S","80S and 80S"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Key fact: eukaryotic cytoplasmic ribosomes are 80S. Ribosomes inside chloroplasts and mitochondria are 70S, like those of prokaryotes.\nSo in a mesophyll cell, the ribosomes in the chloroplast stroma are 70S.\nThe ribosomes free in the cytoplasm are 80S.\nThe trap is option (4), which assumes every ribosome in a eukaryotic cell is 80S.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-140","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":140,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"In a longitudinal section of a mitochondrion, the inner membrane folds inwards towards the matrix. What is the main role of these infoldings?","image":"","options":["They increase the surface area of the inner membrane","They store reserve food as starch granules","They connect the mitochondrion to the endoplasmic reticulum","They form the continuous limiting boundary of the organelle"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The inner mitochondrial membrane folds inwards into the matrix as cristae.\nCristae increase the surface area available for the enzymes of aerobic respiration.\nThe continuous limiting boundary is the outer membrane, so option (4) is wrong.\nMitochondria do not store starch and are not physically joined to the ER.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-141","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":141,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I (Chromosome type)\nA. Metacentric\nB. Sub-metacentric\nC. Acrocentric\nD. Telocentric\nList-II (Arm feature)\nI. One extremely short and one very long arm\nII. Two equal arms\nIII. Centromere at the terminal end\nIV. One shorter and one longer arm\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III","A-II, B-I, C-IV, D-III","A-IV, B-II, C-I, D-III","A-II, B-IV, C-III, D-I"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A. Metacentric: the centromere is in the middle, so the two arms are equal (II).\nB. Sub-metacentric: the centromere is slightly away from the middle, giving one shorter and one longer arm (IV).\nC. Acrocentric: the centromere is close to the end, giving one extremely short and one very long arm (I).\nD. Telocentric: the centromere is at the terminal end (III).\nThe correct matching is A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-142","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":142,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"A microorganism observed under the electron microscope shows the following features:\n(i) a cell wall surrounding the plasma membrane\n(ii) naked circular DNA with no nuclear envelope\n(iii) only 70S ribosomes, with no membrane-bound organelles\n(iv) pigment-containing membranous extensions of the plasma membrane into the cytoplasm\n(v) cyanophycean granules and gas vacuoles in the cytoplasm\nThe organism is most likely:","image":"","options":["Mycoplasma","Chlamydomonas","A cyanobacterium","Euglena"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Naked circular DNA, only 70S ribosomes and no membrane-bound organelles mark the organism as a prokaryote. This rules out Chlamydomonas and Euglena, which are eukaryotes with membrane-bound chloroplasts.\nThe presence of a cell wall rules out mycoplasma, the only wall-less prokaryote.\nPigment-containing chromatophores, cyanophycean granules and gas vacuoles are typical of blue-green algae, i.e. cyanobacteria.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-143","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":143,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of these is not a eukaryote?","image":"","options":["Euglena","Anabaena","Spirogyra","Agaricus"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Key fact: Cyanobacteria (blue-green algae) are prokaryotes. They have no nuclear membrane and no membrane-bound organelles.\nWhy (2) fits: Anabaena is a filamentous cyanobacterium, so it is a prokaryote.\nThe other options are eukaryotes: Euglena is a protist, Spirogyra is a green alga, and Agaricus is a fungus (mushroom).\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 8 (MCQ Q3)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-144","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":144,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following features is common to prokaryotes and many eukaryotes?","image":"","options":["Chromatin material","Cell wall present","Nuclear membrane present","Membranes bound sub-cellular organelles present"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Key fact: Most prokaryotes have a cell wall. So do many eukaryotes, such as plants, fungi and many algae.\nWhy (2) fits: A cell wall is found in both groups.\n(1) fails: Prokaryotes lack histone-bound chromatin; their DNA is naked.\n(3) and (4) fail: Prokaryotes have no nuclear membrane and no membrane-bound organelles.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 8 (MCQ Q6)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-145","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":145,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Arrange the following contributions in correct chronological order:\nP. Virchow explains that new cells arise from pre-existing cells\nQ. Leeuwenhoek sees and describes a live cell\nR. Schwann proposes that animal and plant bodies are made of cells and products of cells\nS. Robert Brown describes the nucleus\nT. Schleiden reports that all plants are composed of different kinds of cells","image":"","options":["S, Q, T, R, P","Q, S, T, R, P","Q, R, T, S, P","Q, S, P, T, R"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Q: Leeuwenhoek saw a live cell (17th century), so this comes first.\nS: Robert Brown described the nucleus (1831).\nT: Schleiden worked on plant cells (1838).\nR: Schwann proposed that plant and animal bodies are made of cells and products of cells (1839).\nP: Virchow showed that cells arise from pre-existing cells (1855).\nSo the order is Q, S, T, R, P.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-146","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":146,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are found in the mitochondrial matrix?\nSingle circular DNA molecule; a few RNA molecules; 80S ribosomes; 70S ribosomes; chlorophyll; components required for protein synthesis","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Single circular DNA molecule: present in the matrix. Correct.\nA few RNA molecules: present. Correct.\n70S ribosomes: present, as in prokaryotes. Correct.\nComponents required for protein synthesis: present. Correct.\n80S ribosomes: absent; these are cytoplasmic ribosomes of eukaryotes. Chlorophyll: absent; it is found only in chloroplast thylakoids.\nSo 4 of the listed items occur in the matrix.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-147","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":147,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are considered part of the endomembrane system?\nEndoplasmic reticulum, Golgi complex, Mitochondria, Lysosomes, Peroxisomes, Vacuoles, Chloroplasts, Ribosomes","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The endomembrane system is made of organelles whose functions are coordinated: the ER, Golgi complex, lysosomes and vacuoles. That gives 4.\nMitochondria, chloroplasts and peroxisomes are membrane-bound, but their functions are not coordinated with these organelles, so they are excluded.\nRibosomes have no membrane at all.\nCount = 4.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-148","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":148,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"The largest isolated single cell is the","image":"","options":["nerve cell of a human","egg of an ostrich","tracheid of a plant","egg of a hen"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT identifies the ostrich egg as the largest isolated single cell.\nA hen's egg is also a single cell, but it is smaller than an ostrich egg.\nNerve cells are among the longest cells, but length is not the same as being the largest isolated cell.\nTracheids are long, dead plant cells and are not isolated single cells.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-149","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":149,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following is described in NCERT as the largest isolated single cell?","image":"","options":["Human nerve cell","Egg of an ostrich","Human red blood cell","Tracheid of a plant"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Key NCERT fact: the egg of an ostrich is the largest isolated single cell.\nNerve cells are among the longest cells, but length is not the same as being the largest isolated cell, so option (1) is the trap.\nA human RBC is only about 7 $\\mu m$ across. Tracheids are elongated dead cells, not the largest single cell.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-150","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":150,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"The widely accepted fluid mosaic model of the plasma membrane was proposed by:","image":"","options":["Schleiden and Schwann (1838)","Watson and Crick (1953)","Singer and Nicolson (1972)","George Palade (1953)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Key fact (NCERT): the fluid mosaic model was proposed by Singer and Nicolson in 1972.\nIt describes the membrane as a quasi-fluid lipid bilayer. Proteins are scattered through it like a mosaic and can move laterally.\nSchleiden and Schwann (1838–39) proposed the cell theory, not a membrane model.\nWatson and Crick (1953) gave the DNA double helix, and Palade is linked with ribosomes. The year 1953 is a distractor here.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-151","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":151,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"The single membrane that bounds the vacuole of a plant cell is called the","image":"","options":["plasmalemma","tonoplast","cisterna","nuclear envelope"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Key fact: the plant vacuole is bounded by a single membrane called the tonoplast.\nThe plasmalemma is the outer plasma membrane of the cell, not the vacuolar membrane.\nCisternae are the flattened sacs of the ER and Golgi.\nThe nuclear envelope is a double membrane around the nucleus.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-152","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":152,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following processes across the plasma membrane require the expenditure of ATP?\n(i) Simple diffusion of a neutral solute from higher to lower concentration\n(ii) Osmosis\n(iii) Carrier protein-assisted movement of a polar molecule from higher to lower concentration\n(iv) Working of the Na$^+$/K$^+$ pump\n(v) Uptake of an ion from lower to higher concentration","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) No ATP: simple diffusion down a gradient is passive.\n(ii) No ATP: osmosis is passive diffusion of water.\n(iii) No ATP: carrier-assisted movement down a gradient is still passive, even though a protein helps.\n(iv) Needs ATP: the Na$^+$/K$^+$ pump moves ions against their gradient.\n(v) Needs ATP: taking up an ion from lower to higher concentration is active transport.\nSo two processes, (iv) and (v), need ATP.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-153","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":153,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements is not true for plasma membrane?","image":"","options":["It is present in both plant and animal cell","Lipid is present as a bilayer in it","Proteins are present integrated as well as loosely associated with the lipid bilayer","Carbohydrate is never found in it"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(1) True: all cells, plant and animal, have a plasma membrane.\n(2) True: phospholipids form a bilayer with polar heads facing outward.\n(3) True: integral proteins are embedded in the bilayer, and peripheral proteins are loosely attached to its surface.\n(4) Not true: carbohydrates are present as glycoproteins and glycolipids on the outer surface. The word 'never' makes this statement false.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 8 (MCQ Q11)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-154","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":154,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"A colourless plastid in a seed cell is found to store proteins. This plastid is a/an:","image":"","options":["Amyloplast","Elaioplast","Aleuroplast","Chromoplast"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Leucoplasts are colourless plastids that store nutrients. There are three types:\nAmyloplasts store starch.\nElaioplasts store oils and fats.\nAleuroplasts store proteins.\nA colourless, protein-storing plastid is therefore an aleuroplast.\nChromoplasts are ruled out because they contain coloured carotenoid pigments.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-155","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":155,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following locations contain 70S ribosomes?\n(i) Cytoplasm of a bacterial cell\n(ii) Matrix of a mitochondrion\n(iii) Stroma of a chloroplast\n(iv) Surface of rough ER in a plant cell\n(v) Cytoplasm of PPLO\n(vi) Free in the cytoplasm of an animal cell","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Bacterial cytoplasm: 70S, since bacteria are prokaryotes.\n(ii) Mitochondrial matrix: 70S.\n(iii) Chloroplast stroma: 70S.\n(iv) Rough ER of a plant cell: 80S, the eukaryotic type.\n(v) PPLO (mycoplasma): 70S, since it is a prokaryote.\n(vi) Free in animal cell cytoplasm: 80S.\nSo four locations contain 70S ribosomes.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-156","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":156,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"The membrane of the human erythrocyte contains approximately","image":"","options":["40 per cent protein and 52 per cent lipids","52 per cent protein and 40 per cent lipids","52 per cent protein and 40 per cent carbohydrates","90 per cent lipids and 10 per cent protein"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT fact: the human erythrocyte membrane has about 52% protein and 40% lipid, so protein exceeds lipid.\nOption (2) matches these values exactly.\nThe tempting trap is option (1), which swaps the two values.\nOption (3) wrongly names carbohydrates as the 40% component.\nOption (4) gives a lipid-dominated ratio, which does not fit RBC data.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-157","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":157,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following is correct:","image":"","options":["Cells of all living organisms have a nucleus.","Both animal and plant cells have a well defined cell wall.","In prokaryotes, there are no membrane bound organelles.","Cells are formed de novo from abiotic materials."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Incorrect: prokaryotes have no true nucleus. Even some eukaryotic cells, such as mature mammalian RBCs, lack one.\n(2) Incorrect: animal cells have no cell wall.\n(3) Correct: prokaryotic cells lack membrane-bound organelles. Their only organelles, ribosomes, are not membrane-bound.\n(4) Incorrect: cells arise only from pre-existing cells (Virchow), not de novo from non-living matter.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 (Exercise Q4)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-158","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":158,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (type of leucoplast) with List-II (stored material):\nList-I\nA. Amyloplast\nB. Elaioplast\nC. Aleuroplast\nList-II\n(i) Proteins\n(ii) Oils and fats\n(iii) Starch","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(i)","A-(i), B-(ii), C-(iii)","A-(iii), B-(i), C-(ii)","A-(ii), B-(iii), C-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A. Amyloplasts store carbohydrates as starch, for example in potato. This gives A-(iii).\nB. Elaioplasts store oils and fats. This gives B-(ii).\nC. Aleuroplasts store proteins. This gives C-(i).\nA common mistake is to swap elaioplasts (oils) and aleuroplasts (proteins). Remember: elaio = oil.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-159","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":159,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Polar molecules require a carrier protein to cross the plasma membrane.\nReason (R): The interior of the lipid bilayer is formed by nonpolar hydrophobic tails.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"R is true: in the lipid bilayer, the polar heads face outward and the nonpolar hydrocarbon tails point inward. The tails form a hydrophobic core.\nA is true: NCERT states that polar molecules cannot pass through this nonpolar bilayer and need a carrier protein to cross it.\nThe hydrophobic interior is exactly what blocks polar molecules, so R explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-160","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":160,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"In the human erythrocyte membrane, what are the approximate proportions of protein and lipid?","image":"","options":["40 per cent protein and 52 per cent lipid","52 per cent protein and 40 per cent lipid","60 per cent protein and 30 per cent lipid","25 per cent protein and 75 per cent lipid"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT states that the human erythrocyte (RBC) membrane has about 52 per cent protein and 40 per cent lipid.\nOption (1) is the common trap, because it swaps the two values.\nThis composition was among the evidence used to study membrane structure.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-161","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":161,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\n(a) Radial spoke of axoneme\n(b) Hub\n(c) Basal body\n(d) Central sheath\nList-II\n(i) Proteinaceous central part of the proximal region of a centriole\n(ii) Centriole-like structure from which a cilium emerges\n(iii) Encloses the central pair of microtubules of the axoneme\n(iv) Joins the central sheath to one tubule of each peripheral doublet","image":"","options":["a-iv, b-i, c-ii, d-iii","a-iii, b-i, c-ii, d-iv","a-iv, b-ii, c-i, d-iii","a-i, b-iv, c-iii, d-ii"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Radial spoke → (iv): in the 9+2 axoneme, radial spokes connect the central sheath to one tubule of each peripheral doublet.\n(b) Hub → (i): the proteinaceous central part of the proximal region of a centriole.\n(c) Basal body → (ii): a centriole-like structure from which cilia and flagella emerge.\n(d) Central sheath → (iii): it encloses the central pair of microtubules.\nThis gives a-iv, b-i, c-ii, d-iii.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-162","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":162,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"An electron micrograph of a transverse section of a eukaryotic flagellum shows a ring of peripheral doublet microtubules. It also shows a central pair of microtubules enclosed in a central sheath. Each peripheral doublet is joined to the central sheath by one radial spoke. How many radial spokes are seen in the section?","image":"","options":["2","9","11","18"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A eukaryotic flagellum has a 9+2 axoneme: nine peripheral doublets surround a central pair of microtubules held in a central sheath.\nOne tubule of each peripheral doublet is joined to the central sheath by one radial spoke.\nNine doublets give $9 \\times 1 = 9$ radial spokes.\n18 is the trap: it is the number of individual peripheral microtubules ($9 \\times 2$), not the number of spokes.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-163","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":163,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Ribosomes were first observed as dense particles under the electron microscope by which scientist?","image":"","options":["Camillo Golgi","George Palade","Robert Brown","Rudolf Virchow"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT states that George Palade (1953) first observed ribosomes as dense particles under the electron microscope.\nCamillo Golgi described the Golgi apparatus.\nRobert Brown discovered the nucleus.\nRudolf Virchow proposed that cells arise from pre-existing cells.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-164","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":164,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following membrane-bound organelles is NOT included in the endomembrane system of a eukaryotic cell?","image":"","options":["Golgi complex","Peroxisome","Vacuole","Endoplasmic reticulum"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Key fact (NCERT): the endomembrane system includes the ER, Golgi complex, lysosomes and vacuoles, because their functions are coordinated.\nPeroxisomes, mitochondria and chloroplasts are also membrane-bound, but their functions are not coordinated with that group. So they are left out.\nThe Golgi complex, vacuole and ER all belong to the system, so they cannot be the answer.\nA common trap: being membrane-bound alone does not make an organelle part of the endomembrane system.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-165","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":165,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following statements about cell walls is incorrect?","image":"","options":["The cell wall is a non-living rigid covering outside the plasma membrane of plants and fungi.","In plants other than algae, the cell wall consists of cellulose, hemicellulose, pectins and proteins.","All prokaryotes possess a cell wall surrounding the cell membrane.","In bacteria, the cell wall determines cell shape and prevents the cell from bursting or collapsing."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: the cell wall is a non-living rigid covering outside the plasma membrane of plants and fungi.\n(2) Correct: in plants other than algae, the wall contains cellulose, hemicellulose, pectins and proteins.\n(3) Incorrect: Mycoplasma is a prokaryote that lacks a cell wall, so 'all prokaryotes' is wrong.\n(4) Correct: in bacteria, the wall gives shape and prevents bursting or collapsing.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-166","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":166,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\nA. Integral proteins\nB. Peripheral proteins\nC. Phospholipids\nD. Na$^+$/K$^+$ pump\nList-II\nI. Arranged as a bilayer in the membrane\nII. Lie on the surface of the membrane\nIII. Partially or totally buried in the membrane\nIV. Moves ions against their concentration gradient using ATP\nChoose the correct answer:","image":"","options":["A-II, B-III, C-I, D-IV","A-III, B-II, C-IV, D-I","A-III, B-II, C-I, D-IV","A-I, B-II, C-III, D-IV"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A–III: integral proteins are partially or totally buried in the membrane.\nB–II: peripheral proteins lie on the surface of the membrane.\nC–I: phospholipids are arranged as a bilayer.\nD–IV: the Na$^+$/K$^+$ pump moves ions against their gradient using ATP.\nOption (1) is the trap, because it swaps the two protein types.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-167","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":167,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Cells that are actively carrying out protein synthesis have larger and more numerous nucleoli.\nReason (R): The nucleolus is a site of active ribosomal RNA synthesis.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: NCERT notes that cells actively making protein have larger and more numerous nucleoli.\nR is true: the nucleolus is a site of active ribosomal RNA (rRNA) synthesis.\nLink: proteins are made on ribosomes, and ribosomes are built from rRNA and proteins. A cell making a lot of protein needs many ribosomes, so it needs more rRNA. Its nucleoli therefore become larger and more numerous.\nSo R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-168","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":168,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Microbodies are membrane-bound minute vesicles containing various enzymes, present in both plant and animal cells.\nStatement II: In a single human cell, approximately two metre long DNA is distributed among its twenty-three chromosomes.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. NCERT describes microbodies as membrane-bound minute vesicles that contain various enzymes and occur in both plant and animal cells.\nStatement II is incorrect. The roughly 2 m of DNA in a human cell is distributed among 46 chromosomes (23 pairs), not 23.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-169","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":169,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The cytoskeleton is made of microtubules, microfilaments and intermediate filaments present in the cytoplasm.\nStatement II: The cytoskeleton is a network of membrane-bound lipid tubules that mainly synthesises steroid hormones.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. The cytoskeleton is a network of proteinaceous filaments in the cytoplasm: microtubules, microfilaments and intermediate filaments. It provides mechanical support, motility and maintenance of cell shape.\nStatement II is incorrect. The cytoskeleton is not made of membrane-bound lipid tubules. Synthesis of lipids and steroid hormones is a function of the smooth ER.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-170","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":170,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Match the following:\nColumn I: (a) Cristae, (b) Cisternae, (c) Thylakoids\nColumn II: (i) Flat membranous sacs in stroma, (ii) Infoldings in mitochondria, (iii) Disc-shaped sacs in Golgi apparatus","image":"","options":["(a)-(i), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(a) Cristae are infoldings of the inner mitochondrial membrane, so (a)-(ii).\n(b) Cisternae are the flat, disc-shaped sacs stacked in the Golgi apparatus, so (b)-(iii).\n(c) Thylakoids are flat membranous sacs found in the chloroplast stroma, so (c)-(i).\nOnly option (2) gives (a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i).\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 (Exercise Q3) (converted to MCQ)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-171","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":171,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In eukaryotic plant cells, ribosomes are found not only in the cytoplasm but also inside chloroplasts and mitochondria.\nStatement II: Prokaryotic cells lack all types of organelles, so no protein-synthesising particles are present in them.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. In plant cells, ribosomes occur free in the cytoplasm and on rough ER. Chloroplasts and mitochondria also contain their own 70S ribosomes.\nStatement II is incorrect. Prokaryotes lack only membrane-bound organelles. They do have 70S ribosomes, which carry out protein synthesis.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-172","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":172,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"The nuclear envelope has two parallel membranes with a space between them called the perinuclear space. According to NCERT, what is the approximate width of this space?","image":"","options":["1 to 5 nm","100 to 500 nm","10 to 50 nm","0.5 to 1.0 µm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Electron microscopy shows that the nuclear envelope has two parallel membranes separated by the perinuclear space.\nNCERT gives the width of this space as 10 to 50 nm.\nThe value 0.5 to 1.0 µm is a trap: it is the diameter of Golgi cisternae.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-173","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":173,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"A common characteristic feature of plant sieve tube cells and most of mammalian erythrocytes is","image":"","options":["Absence of mitochondria","Presence of cell wall","Presence of haemoglobin","Absence of nucleus"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Key fact: During maturation, sieve tube elements lose their nucleus. Most mammalian RBCs also lose theirs, making them biconcave.\nWhy (4) fits: Lacking a nucleus is the feature both cell types share.\n(1) fails: Sieve tubes keep their mitochondria, even though RBCs lose them.\n(2) fails: Only plant cells have a cell wall.\n(3) fails: Only erythrocytes contain haemoglobin.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 8 (MCQ Q1)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-174","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":174,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"The nucleolus is primarily a site for:","image":"","options":["Active ribosomal RNA synthesis","Synthesis of steroid hormones","Packaging of glycoproteins","Synthesis of histone proteins"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The nucleolus is a non-membrane-bound structure inside the nucleus. Its main role is active synthesis of ribosomal RNA (rRNA).\nSteroid hormones are made in the smooth endoplasmic reticulum, not the nucleolus.\nGlycoproteins are formed and packaged in the Golgi apparatus.\nHistones are proteins, so they are made on ribosomes in the cytoplasm.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-175","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":175,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"In a chloroplast, which structures are the flat membranous tubules that connect the thylakoids of different grana?","image":"","options":["Cristae","Cisternae","Stroma lamellae","Plasmodesmata"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Stroma lamellae are flat membranous tubules that connect the thylakoids of different grana.\nCristae are infoldings of the inner mitochondrial membrane, not chloroplast structures.\nCisternae are the flattened sacs of the Golgi apparatus and ER.\nPlasmodesmata are cytoplasmic connections between neighbouring plant cells.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-176","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":176,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the nuclear envelope is incorrect?","image":"","options":["Nuclear pores are formed by the fusion of the two membranes of the envelope","The outer nuclear membrane usually remains continuous with the endoplasmic reticulum","The inner nuclear membrane bears ribosomes on its surface facing the nucleoplasm","Mature erythrocytes of many mammals and sieve tube cells of vascular plants lack a nucleus"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: nuclear pores form where the inner and outer membranes fuse.\n(2) Correct: the outer membrane is usually continuous with the ER.\n(3) Incorrect: ribosomes are borne on the outer membrane, which faces the cytoplasm. The inner membrane faces the nucleoplasm and has no ribosomes.\n(4) Correct: mature erythrocytes of many mammals and sieve tube cells lack a nucleus.\nThe incorrect statement is (3).\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-177","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":177,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"A bacterial flagellum has three parts: filament, hook and basal body. Which part is the longest and extends from the cell surface to the outside?","image":"","options":["Basal body","Hook","Filament","Pilus"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT fact: a bacterial flagellum has three parts, the filament, the hook and the basal body.\nThe filament is the longest part and extends from the cell surface to the outside, so it fits the question.\nThe hook and the basal body are short parts near the cell surface.\nThe hook joins the filament to the basal body, and the basal body is embedded in the cell envelope.\nA pilus is a separate surface structure, not a part of the flagellum.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-178","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":178,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"The inner mitochondrial membrane forms infoldings called cristae. What is the main significance of these infoldings?","image":"","options":["They divide the matrix into separate compartments for DNA storage","They increase the surface area of the inner membrane","They connect the mitochondrion with the endoplasmic reticulum","They form the outer limiting boundary of the organelle"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Cristae are infoldings of the inner mitochondrial membrane that project towards the matrix.\nTheir main significance is that they increase the surface area of the inner membrane.\nOption (4) is the tempting wrong choice. The continuous outer limiting boundary is formed by the outer membrane, not by the cristae.\nCristae also do not separate the matrix into compartments for DNA storage.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-179","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":179,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"In animal cells, lipid-like steroidal hormones are synthesised mainly in the","image":"","options":["rough endoplasmic reticulum","Golgi apparatus","smooth endoplasmic reticulum","lysosomes"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Key fact: the smooth ER (SER) has no ribosomes and is the major site of lipid synthesis.\nIn animal cells, lipid-like steroidal hormones are made in the SER.\nThe tempting option, RER, carries ribosomes and is mainly concerned with protein synthesis and secretion, not steroids.\nThe Golgi packages and modifies materials. Lysosomes digest materials.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-180","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":180,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are membrane-bound organelles of a eukaryotic cell?\nEndoplasmic reticulum, Golgi complex, Ribosome, Lysosome, Centrosome, Mitochondrion, Microbody, Vacuole","image":"","options":["4","5","6","7"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Endoplasmic reticulum: membrane-bound, a network of membranous tubules and cisternae.\nGolgi complex: membrane-bound, made of flattened cisternae.\nLysosome, microbody and vacuole: each is bounded by a single membrane (the vacuole's is the tonoplast).\nMitochondrion: membrane-bound, with a double membrane.\nRibosome and centrosome: not membrane-bound; they are made of RNA and protein, and of microtubule-based centrioles.\nThat gives 6 membrane-bound organelles.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-181","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":181,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (cell type) with List-II (shape):\nList-I\nA. Red blood cell\nB. White blood cell\nC. Columnar epithelial cell\nD. Nerve cell\nList-II\nI. Amoeboid\nII. Branched and long\nIII. Round and biconcave\nIV. Long and narrow","image":"","options":["A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-I, B-III, C-IV, D-II","A-III, B-I, C-II, D-IV","A-IV, B-I, C-III, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A. RBC: round and biconcave, which suits gas exchange (III).\nB. WBC: amoeboid, so it can squeeze through vessel walls (I).\nC. Columnar epithelial cell: long and narrow (IV).\nD. Nerve cell: branched and long, which suits conducting impulses (II).\nThis gives A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II, which is option (1).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-182","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":182,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following structures is present in both prokaryotic and eukaryotic cells?","image":"","options":["Golgi complex","Mitochondrion","Ribosome","Nuclear envelope"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Ribosomes are non-membrane bound organelles found in all cells. Prokaryotes have 70S ribosomes and eukaryotic cytoplasm has 80S ribosomes.\nProkaryotes lack membrane-bound organelles, so they have no Golgi complex and no mitochondria.\nProkaryotes also lack a nuclear envelope, because their DNA lies in a nucleoid.\nSo the ribosome is the only structure listed that both cell types share.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-183","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":183,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following is the smallest cell, and what is its approximate length?","image":"","options":["Bacterium, about 3 $\\mu m$","Mycoplasma, about 0.3 $\\mu m$","Human red blood cell, about 7.0 $\\mu m$","Virus, about 0.3 $\\mu m$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT states that Mycoplasma, about 0.3 $\\mu m$ long, is the smallest cell.\nBacteria are larger, about 3–5 $\\mu m$, and human RBCs are about 7.0 $\\mu m$.\nOption (4) is the tempting trap. Viruses are acellular, so they are not cells at all and cannot be the smallest cell.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-184","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":184,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The axoneme of a cilium has nine radial spokes.\nReason (R): The central sheath around the central pair of microtubules is joined to one tubule of each of the nine peripheral doublets by a radial spoke.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: NCERT states that the axoneme of a cilium or flagellum has nine radial spokes.\nR is true: the central sheath around the central pair of microtubules is linked by one radial spoke to one tubule of each peripheral doublet.\nThere are nine peripheral doublets and each gets one spoke, so there are nine spokes. R therefore directly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-185","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":185,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following statements about mitochondria and plastids is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["A typical mitochondrion has an average diameter of about 0.5 µm and a length of 1.0–4.1 µm","Each cell of Chlamydomonas has 20–40 chloroplasts, like a mesophyll cell","The inner and outer mitochondrial membranes divide its lumen into an outer and an inner aqueous compartment","Plastids occur in all plant cells and also in euglenoides"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: a typical mitochondrion has an average diameter of about 0.5 µm and a length of 1.0–4.1 µm.\n(2) Incorrect: Chlamydomonas has a single chloroplast per cell. The figure of 20–40 chloroplasts applies to mesophyll cells.\n(3) Correct: the two membranes divide the lumen into an outer and an inner (matrix) compartment.\n(4) Correct: plastids occur in all plant cells and also in euglenoides.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-186","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":186,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (plastid) with List-II (stored material or pigment):\nList-I\nA. Amyloplast\nB. Elaioplast\nC. Aleuroplast\nD. Chromoplast\nList-II\nI. Proteins\nII. Carotene and xanthophylls\nIII. Starch\nIV. Oils and fats","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-I, B-IV, C-III, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A. Amyloplast stores starch (for example in potato): A-III.\nB. Elaioplast stores oils and fats: B-IV.\nC. Aleuroplast stores proteins: C-I.\nD. Chromoplast contains fat-soluble carotenoids such as carotene and xanthophylls: D-II.\nAvoid the common trap of swapping elaioplast and aleuroplast. 'Aleuro' links to aleurone, the protein layer.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-187","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":187,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Who proposed the fluid mosaic model of plasma membrane?","image":"","options":["Benda","Schleiden and Schwann","Singer and Nicolson","Robert Brown"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Key fact: Singer and Nicolson proposed the fluid mosaic model in 1972. It describes the membrane as a quasi-fluid lipid bilayer with proteins moving laterally within it.\nOther options: Benda named mitochondria. Schleiden and Schwann gave the cell theory. Robert Brown discovered the nucleus.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 8 (MCQ Q7)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-188","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":188,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The cell is the fundamental structural and functional unit of all living organisms.\nReason (R): Anything less than a complete structure of a cell does not ensure independent living.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. All organisms are composed of cells, and the cell is their basic unit of structure and function.\nR is true. NCERT notes that anything less than a complete cell cannot live independently, which is why unicellular organisms can survive alone.\nR explains A. The cell counts as the fundamental unit precisely because it is the smallest structure capable of independent life.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-189","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":189,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements comparing the axoneme of a cilium with a centriole is incorrect?","image":"","options":["The axoneme has nine peripheral doublets and a central pair of microtubules enclosed by a central sheath","In the axoneme, nine radial spokes connect the central sheath to one tubule of each peripheral doublet","In a centriole, each of the nine evenly spaced peripheral fibrils of tubulin is a doublet, joined to a central hub by radial spokes","The two centrioles of a centrosome lie perpendicular to each other and can form the basal body of a cilium"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: the axoneme has a 9+2 arrangement, with the central pair enclosed by a central sheath.\n(2) Correct: radial spokes connect the central sheath to one tubule of each peripheral doublet.\n(3) Incorrect: each of the nine peripheral fibrils of a centriole is a triplet, not a doublet. The triplets form a cartwheel around a central hub. Doublets belong to the axoneme of cilia and flagella.\n(4) Correct: the two centrioles of a centrosome lie perpendicular to each other and form basal bodies.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-190","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":190,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In the plasma membrane, phospholipids are arranged so that their polar heads face the outer sides of the bilayer and their hydrophobic tails face the interior.\nReason (R): This arrangement keeps the nonpolar hydrocarbon tails protected from the aqueous environment.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. In the bilayer, the polar (hydrophilic) heads of the phospholipids face outward on both sides.\nThe nonpolar (hydrophobic) hydrocarbon tails face each other in the interior.\nR is true. This orientation keeps the nonpolar tails away from the surrounding water.\nR correctly explains A, because the bilayer arranges itself this way precisely to shield the hydrophobic tails from the aqueous environment.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-191","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":191,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"A secretory glycoprotein is synthesised by ribosomes on the endoplasmic reticulum and finally released outside the cell. Which sequence correctly traces its path?","image":"","options":["RER → transport vesicle → trans face of Golgi → cis face of Golgi → secretory vesicle → plasma membrane","RER → transport vesicle → cis face of Golgi → trans face of Golgi → secretory vesicle → plasma membrane","SER → transport vesicle → cis face of Golgi → trans face of Golgi → lysosome → plasma membrane","Cis face of Golgi → RER → trans face of Golgi → secretory vesicle → plasma membrane"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Secretory proteins are made by ribosomes bound to the rough ER (RER), not the SER, which lacks ribosomes.\nThey are carried in transport vesicles to the cis (forming) face of the Golgi.\nThey are modified, including glycosylation, as they move to the trans (maturing) face.\nThe trans face releases secretory vesicles, which fuse with the plasma membrane by exocytosis.\nOption (1) reverses the cis-to-trans direction. Option (3) starts at the SER and ends in lysosomes. Option (4) begins at the Golgi instead of the RER.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-192","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":192,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the plasma membrane is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["The ratio of protein to lipid in the membrane is the same in all cell types","The ability of proteins to move laterally within the bilayer is measured as membrane fluidity","The fluid nature of the membrane is important for endocytosis, secretion and formation of intercellular junctions","Besides phospholipids and proteins, the membrane also contains cholesterol and carbohydrates"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(1) Incorrect: NCERT states that the protein-to-lipid ratio varies considerably between cell types. It is not constant.\n(2) Correct: membrane fluidity is measured as the ability of proteins to move laterally within the bilayer.\n(3) Correct: fluidity supports endocytosis, secretion, cell growth, cell division and the formation of intercellular junctions.\n(4) Correct: besides phospholipids and proteins, the membrane contains cholesterol and carbohydrates.\nThe question asks for the INCORRECT statement, which is (1).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"hard"}]});
